consiSt("oh",{"courts":[{"l":"HIGHEST COURT","n":"Supreme Court of Ohio","d":"Chief Justice and six justices. Hears appeals of right (for example, cases that began in a court of appeals and death-penalty cases), discretionary jurisdictional appeals (substantial constitutional question, felony, or public or great general interest), certified conflicts between courts of appeals, questions certified by federal courts, agency appeals (such as the Board of Tax Appeals and Public Utilities Commission), and original actions. It also regulates the bar, pro hac vice registration, and lawyer discipline, and the Chief Justice decides affidavits of disqualification against lower-court judges."},{"l":"INTERMEDIATE","n":"Courts of Appeals (twelve appellate districts)","d":"Three-judge panels hear appeals of right from final orders of trial courts in the district. Papers are filed with the clerk of the court of appeals in the county where the case began. Each district adopts its own local rules (for example on e-filing, docketing statements, and the accelerated calendar)."},{"l":"TRIAL COURTS","n":"Courts of Common Pleas","d":"One in each county; the general-jurisdiction trial court. Many counties have separate general, domestic relations, probate, and juvenile divisions. Civ.R. 73 (probate) and 75 (domestic relations) add special procedures."},{"l":"LIMITED JURISDICTION","n":"Municipal and County Courts; Court of Claims; Mayor’s Courts","d":"Municipal and county courts handle smaller civil claims, small claims, evictions, misdemeanors, and traffic. The Court of Claims hears suits against the State. Mayor’s courts hear limited ordinance and traffic matters and are not courts of record."}],"slapp":[{"t":"Statute: Ohio’s Uniform Public Expression Protection Act (effective April 9, 2025)","d":"Ohio adopted the Uniform Public Expression Protection Act (UPEPA) as R.C. Chapter 2747 (S.B. 237, 135th General Assembly), effective April 9, 2025. It applies to civil actions filed, and claims asserted, on or after that date.\nIt covers claims based on a person’s (1) communication in a legislative, executive, judicial, administrative, or other governmental proceeding; (2) communication on an issue under consideration in such a proceeding; or (3) exercise of speech, press, assembly, petition, or association rights under the U.S. or Ohio Constitution on a matter of public concern.\nThe General Assembly says it intends substantive immunity from suit, not just immunity from liability. Courts must construe the Act broadly and consider uniformity with other UPEPA states.","c":"R.C. 2747.01(B), (E); R.C. 2747.06","v":true},{"t":"What the Act does not cover","d":"Exempt: (1) claims against a governmental unit or its employee or agent acting in an official capacity; (2) government enforcement actions to protect against an imminent threat to public health or safety; (3) claims against a person primarily engaged in selling or leasing goods or services when the claim arises from communications about that sale or lease (creative, journalistic, political, and artistic works are not ‘goods or services’); (4) survivorship, bodily-injury, and wrongful-death claims, and statements about them.\nThe responding party bears the burden of proving an exemption applies.","c":"R.C. 2747.01(A)(1), (C); R.C. 2747.04(C)(2)","v":true},{"t":"Procedure: 60-day motion, automatic stay, 60-day hearing, 60-day ruling","d":"File a ‘motion for expedited relief’ within 60 days after service of the complaint, counterclaim, cross-claim, third-party claim, or other pleading asserting the claim; the court may extend this for good cause.\nFiling stays all other proceedings between the movant and the responding party, including discovery and pending motions. The stay lasts until 30 days after the ruling or the end of any appeal, whichever is later; during an appeal, everything in the action is stayed for all parties. The court may allow limited, specific discovery needed to meet a burden, may hear unrelated motions or public-safety injunction requests for good cause, and fee motions are not stayed.\nThe hearing must be held within 60 days after filing (later if discovery is allowed or for good cause), and the court must rule within 60 days after the hearing.","c":"R.C. 2747.02; R.C. 2747.03; R.C. 2747.04(A), (D)","v":true},{"t":"Standard: three steps; Rule 56-type evidence","d":"The court considers the pleadings, motion, response, and any evidence usable on summary judgment under Civ.R. 56. It must dismiss with prejudice if (1) the movant shows the claim is based on protected activity; (2) the respondent fails to show an exemption; and (3) either the respondent fails to establish a prima facie case on each essential element, or the movant shows failure to state a claim or no genuine issue of material fact and entitlement to judgment as a matter of law.\nA voluntary dismissal without prejudice does not defeat the movant’s right to a ruling for fee purposes; a dismissal with prejudice makes the movant the prevailing party.","c":"R.C. 2747.04(B), (C), (E)","v":true},{"t":"Fees: mandatory for a winning movant; only for frivolous motions against one","d":"If the motion is granted, the court must award the movant reasonable attorney’s fees, court costs, and other reasonable litigation expenses, and may not reduce them because the representation was pro bono or contingent.\nIf the motion is denied and the court finds it was frivolous conduct under R.C. 2323.51, the respondent gets its fees and expenses, but only after any appeal affirming the denial.","c":"R.C. 2747.05(A)-(B)","v":true},{"t":"Appeal: denial is a final order; 30 days","d":"A denial is a final, appealable order, giving the movant an interlocutory appeal. The appeal must be filed within 30 days after entry of the order. R.C. 2505.02(B)(9) separately lists an order denying a motion for expedited relief under R.C. 2747.04 as a final order.\nThe statute gives no corresponding immediate appeal from a grant; a grant that disposes of fewer than all claims is subject to the usual final-order and Civ.R. 54(B) rules.","c":"R.C. 2747.05(C); R.C. 2505.02(B)(9); Civ.R. 54(B)","v":true}],"oos":[{"t":"Pro hac vice: register with the Supreme Court first ($500), then move in the tribunal","d":"Before any Ohio tribunal (court, agency acting adjudicatively, or the Board on the Unauthorized Practice of Law) can grant permission, the lawyer must apply to the Supreme Court Office of Bar Admissions, pay a $500 registration fee, and obtain a certificate of pro hac vice registration. The application lists addresses, every jurisdiction of licensure with dates and registration numbers, an affidavit of no disbarment and any suspension or resignation with discipline pending, and a promise to follow Ohio law, the tribunal’s rules, the Ohio Rules of Professional Conduct, and the Gov.Bar.R.\nThe motion to the tribunal attaches the certificate, repeats that information, states the lawyer has not exceeded the annual cap, and names an active Ohio lawyer in good standing (with registration number) who has agreed to associate. Serve the motion on all known parties; any party may object, and the tribunal may hold a hearing.\nIf an appearance is needed fewer than 5 business days after the application, the lawyer may move ‘instanter’ with a time-stamped copy of the pending application.","c":"Gov.Bar R. XII, Sections 1, 2(A)(3), 2(A)(6), 2(C)-(E), 3","v":true},{"t":"Who qualifies, and who is barred","d":"Eligible: lawyers admitted in the highest court of any U.S. state, territory, or D.C., or in a foreign country’s courts, who are in good standing and (a) do not live in or work regularly from an Ohio office; (b) hold Ohio corporate (in-house) registration; (c) live in Ohio but lawfully practice from offices elsewhere, including lawful remote practice; (d) maintain an Ohio presence under Prof.Cond.R. 5.5(d)(2) or (d)(4); or (e) moved to Ohio within the last 120 days and have a pending admission application.\nBarred for 5 years: anyone who took and failed the Ohio bar exam, was denied admission without examination, or was denied admission on character and fitness grounds.","c":"Gov.Bar R. XII, Section 2(A), (A)(1)-(2)","v":true},{"t":"Cap: three new proceedings per calendar year","d":"A lawyer may be admitted pro hac vice in no more than three proceedings in the calendar year of the application. A proceeding carried into later years does not count again, and an appeal, agency appeal, transfer, or consolidation of a case the lawyer was already in does not count. Joining a proceeding for the first time at any stage does count.","c":"Gov.Bar R. XII, Section 2(A)(5), (A)(6)(d)","v":true},{"t":"After admission: 30-day notice and annual renewal, or automatic exclusion","d":"Within 30 days after any tribunal grants permission, file a Notice of Permission to Appear Pro Hac Vice (with the order) with the Office of Bar Admissions. If the case is still pending on January 1, pay a renewal fee equal to the $500 registration fee and file an updated form within 30 days after the year begins; if the case ended or the lawyer withdrew, tell the Office by that deadline.\nMissing either step means automatic exclusion from practice in Ohio, and the Office notifies every tribunal by certified mail. Reinstatement requires a petition. The lawyer must also tell each tribunal of any discipline imposed after admission.","c":"Gov.Bar R. XII, Sections 2(B), 4, 5, 6","v":true},{"t":"Supreme Court of Ohio: separate pro hac vice motion and deadlines","d":"To file anything beyond what is needed to perfect an appeal, or to argue, a lawyer must be registered for active Ohio status or admitted pro hac vice under Gov.Bar R. XII and S.Ct.Prac.R. 2.02. The motion attaches the registration certificate and the XII(2)(A)(3) information.\nIf a non-Ohio lawyer perfects an appeal without moving, the motion is due within 30 days after the appeal is filed; to argue, file it at least 30 days before oral argument. The Clerk refuses late or noncompliant motions. Within 30 days of admission, file the notice with the Office of Attorney Services, and renew each January for pending cases.","c":"S.Ct.Prac.R. 2.01(A)(1), 2.02(B)-(D)","v":true},{"t":"Limited-scope appearances: Notice of Limited Appearance","d":"Ohio allows limited-scope representation if the limitation is reasonable and communicated to the client, preferably in writing; unlike ABA Model Rule 1.2(c), client informed consent is not required.\nIn court, the lawyer files and serves a ‘Notice of Limited Appearance’ describing the scope and stating that the client authorized it, before or at the appearance. Other parties must then serve both the lawyer and the client. The lawyer gets out by filing a ‘Notice of Completion of Limited Appearance’; if no one objects within 10 days of service, no court entry is needed.","c":"Prof.Cond.R. 1.2(c); Civ.R. 3(B); Civ.R. 5(B)(1)","v":true},{"t":"Temporary practice without pro hac vice","d":"Lawyers admitted and in good standing elsewhere who regularly practice may provide temporary services in Ohio: with an actively participating Ohio lawyer; for matters related to a pending or expected proceeding where they are or expect to be authorized to appear; for arbitration, mediation, or other ADR related to their home practice (unless the forum requires pro hac vice); or for negotiations and other non-litigation work related to their home practice.","c":"Prof.Cond.R. 5.5(c)","v":true},{"t":"E-filing: no single statewide trial-court system","d":"The Civil Rules let each court adopt electronic filing by court order or local rule, which must cover electronic signatures, the days and hours filings are received and when they count as filed, and fee payment. The courts of appeals follow the same model under App.R. 13(B)(2). Check each county’s and district’s local rules for portal registration and whether pro hac vice counsel can register.\nThe Supreme Court of Ohio has its own E-Filing Portal. Its use is optional, filings must be PDFs, and documents received after 11:59:59 p.m. Columbus time count as filed the next business day. The portal’s receipt confirmation is not acceptance; the Clerk e-mails whether the filing was accepted.","c":"Civ.R. 5(E); App.R. 13(B)(2); S.Ct.Prac.R. 2.01(A)(3), 3.02(A)(3)","v":true}],"ethics":[{"t":"Governing code: Ohio Rules of Professional Conduct (as amended effective January 1, 2026)","d":"Ohio follows the ABA Model Rules numbering, with official ‘Comparison to ABA Model Rules’ notes after many rules explaining Ohio’s changes. Discipline is governed by Gov.Bar R. V.","c":"Ohio Rules of Professional Conduct (eff. Feb. 1, 2007, as amended eff. Jan. 1, 2026)","v":true},{"t":"Malpractice insurance notice signed by the client","d":"A lawyer who lacks professional liability insurance of at least $100,000 per occurrence and $300,000 aggregate, or whose coverage ends, must tell the client at engagement (or when coverage ends) on the separate form that follows the rule, signed by the client. Keep the signed notice for 5 years after the representation ends. A lawyer in a fee division must give the notice before the client is asked to agree to the division. The Model Rules have no such provision.","c":"Prof.Cond.R. 1.4(c)","v":true},{"t":"Fees: ‘clearly excessive’ standard; contingent-fee closing statement","d":"Ohio forbids illegal or clearly excessive fees, rather than the Model Rule’s ‘unreasonable’. Contingent-fee agreements must be in a writing signed by both the client and the lawyer, setting out percentages for settlement, trial, or appeal and how expenses are handled. When the lawyer disburses the recovery, the lawyer must give the client a closing statement showing how the fee was computed and what costs were deducted.","c":"Prof.Cond.R. 1.5(a), (c)(1)-(2) and Comparison to ABA Model Rules","v":true},{"t":"Broader confidentiality exceptions","d":"Beyond the Model Rule exceptions, a lawyer may reveal information (including privileged information) to the extent reasonably necessary to prevent the commission of any crime by the client or another person, and to mitigate substantial financial injury from a client’s illegal or fraudulent act that used the lawyer’s services.","c":"Prof.Cond.R. 1.6(b)(2)-(3)","v":true},{"t":"Duty to report other lawyers and judges","d":"A lawyer with unprivileged knowledge of a violation that raises a question about any lawyer’s honesty, trustworthiness, or fitness must report it. Any ethics violation by a judge must also be reported, broader than the Model Rule’s ‘substantial question’ standard.","c":"Prof.Cond.R. 8.3(a)-(b) and Comparison to ABA Model Rules","v":true},{"t":"Other notable departures","d":"Rule 1.2(d) and 4.1(b) use ‘illegal’ rather than ‘criminal’. Rule 1.8(f)(4) adds a Statement of Insured Client’s Rights for insurance defense. Rule 1.10(c)-(d) allows screening of lateral hires except where the lawyer had a substantial role in the same matter. Rule 1.13 omits the Model Rule’s reporting-out provision. Rule 3.5(a)(2) bars giving or lending anything of more than de minimis value to a judicial officer, juror, or prospective juror. Rule 7.2 omits Model Rule 7.2(b)(4), so reciprocal referral agreements are prohibited. Rule 8.4(g) bars discrimination prohibited by law in a professional capacity, and 8.4(h) bars any other conduct adversely reflecting on fitness.","c":"Prof.Cond.R. 1.2, 1.8, 1.10, 1.13, 3.5, 4.1, 7.2, 8.4 and Comparisons to ABA Model Rules","v":true}],"style":"State of Ohio","home":"https://www.supremecourt.ohio.gov/","unique":{"mines":[{"t":"Filing is not commencement unless service is obtained within one year","d":"An Ohio action is ‘commenced’ by filing only if service is obtained on a named defendant within one year of filing. Late service can mean the limitations period was never stopped.","c":"Civ.R. 3(A)"},{"t":"Do not state a dollar amount above $25,000","d":"A pleading seeking more than $25,000 must say so but may not state the amount.","c":"Civ.R. 8(A)"},{"t":"Medical-claim complaints need an affidavit of merit","d":"A complaint with a medical, dental, optometric, or chiropractic claim must include an affidavit of merit from a qualified expert for each defendant who needs expert proof. Any motion to extend must be filed with the complaint, and extensions are capped at 90 days except as the rule allows.","c":"Civ.R. 10(D)(2)"},{"t":"Continuance motions must be signed by the client too","d":"A motion to continue a trial or hearing must be written, state the reason, and be endorsed by the party as well as counsel, unless the judge waives this for good cause.","c":"Civ.R. 6(B)(2)(b)"},{"t":"Object to the magistrate within 14 days or lose the issue","d":"Failing to file specific objections to a magistrate’s decision within 14 days forfeits appellate review of the adopted findings and conclusions, except for plain error.","c":"Civ.R. 53(D)(3)(b)(i), (iv)"},{"t":"30 days to appeal, with no extension","d":"The notice of appeal is due in the trial court within 30 days of entry of judgment, and the court of appeals cannot extend it. The clock starts later only if the clerk failed to serve notice of the judgment within 3 days, or after listed post-judgment motions (Civ.R. 50(B), 59, magistrate objections, findings requests, attorney-fee and prejudgment-interest motions) are resolved. Civ.R. 60(B) motions are not on the tolling list.","c":"App.R. 4(A), (B)(2); App.R. 14(B)"},{"t":"Assignments of error must be stated and argued separately","d":"The court of appeals may disregard an assignment of error that does not identify the error in the record or is not argued separately in the brief.","c":"App.R. 12(A)(2); App.R. 16(A)(3), (7)"},{"t":"Supreme Court deadlines are hard: 45 days, no extensions, memorandum filed with the notice","d":"A discretionary appeal requires both a notice of appeal and a 15-page memorandum in support of jurisdiction within 45 days of the court of appeals’ entry. The Clerk refuses late filings, and the Court will not extend this time. A motion to certify a conflict in the court of appeals does not extend the 45 days.","c":"S.Ct.Prac.R. 3.02(B), 3.03(B)(1), 7.01(A)(1), 7.02(B); App.R. 25(A)"},{"t":"Pro hac vice is capped and has hidden renewal deadlines","d":"Three new proceedings per calendar year, $500 registration before moving, a 30-day post-grant notice, and a $500 renewal each January for pending cases. Missing a notice or renewal means automatic exclusion.","c":"Gov.Bar R. XII, Sections 2(A)(3), 2(A)(5), 4, 5"},{"t":"Judge disqualification goes to Columbus, at least 7 days out","d":"The affidavit of disqualification is filed with the Clerk of the Supreme Court, not the trial court, at least 7 calendar days before the next hearing. It must be notarized and served, or the Clerk rejects it.","c":"R.C. 2701.03(B), (C)(2)"},{"t":"Two notice dismissals operate as an adjudication on the merits","d":"A plaintiff’s Civ.R. 41(A)(1) notice dismissal is without prejudice, but a notice dismissal of a claim the plaintiff has already dismissed once in any court is an adjudication on the merits.","c":"Civ.R. 41(A)(1)"},{"t":"UPEPA motion due 60 days after service","d":"The anti-SLAPP motion for expedited relief must be filed within 60 days after service of the pleading (extendable for good cause). Filing stays discovery, and a granted motion carries mandatory fees.","c":"R.C. 2747.02, 2747.03, 2747.05(A)"}],"judge":[{"t":"No peremptory substitution of judge","d":"The collected Civil Rules and statutes contain no right to remove a trial judge without cause. Removal is by affidavit of disqualification decided by the Supreme Court’s Chief Justice, or by the judge’s own recusal.","c":"Civ.R. 1-86; R.C. 2701.03 (no peremptory procedure found)","v":true},{"t":"Affidavit of disqualification goes to the Supreme Court Clerk, at least 7 days before the next hearing","d":"A party or counsel claiming a common pleas judge is interested, related, biased, or otherwise disqualified files a sworn affidavit with the Clerk of the Supreme Court of Ohio, not with the trial court. File it at least 7 calendar days before the next scheduled hearing. It must include specific allegations and supporting facts, a notary’s jurat, a certificate of service on the judge and all parties, and the next hearing date (or a statement that none is set). The Clerk must reject untimely or noncompliant affidavits.\nAn accepted affidavit stops the judge from presiding until the Chief Justice (or a designated justice) rules, except that the judge may rule on matters not affecting substantive rights and certain temporary domestic and juvenile orders, and may preside if the affidavit was untimely. A repeat affidavit by the same party after a denial does not stop the judge. If disqualification is granted, the case is reassigned. The same procedure covers municipal and county court judges.","c":"R.C. 2701.03(A)-(E); R.C. 2701.031","v":true},{"t":"Recusal standard for all judges","d":"Judges must disqualify themselves whenever impartiality might reasonably be questioned. Examples: personal bias or knowledge of disputed facts; a spouse, domestic partner, or relative within the third degree who is a party, lawyer, interested person, or likely material witness; family economic interests; public statements committing to a result; prior work on the matter as a lawyer or government official; or having presided over it in another court.","c":"Ohio Code of Judicial Conduct, Jud.Cond.R. 2.11(A) (as amended Feb. 12, 2026)","v":true},{"t":"Recusal of a Supreme Court justice","d":"Request it by a letter to the Clerk of the Supreme Court, with an affidavit stating the specific basis and supporting facts, served on all parties. File promptly, and in argued cases at least 15 days before oral argument absent leave. The named justice responds in writing. Amici may not file recusal requests.","c":"S.Ct.Prac.R. 4.04","v":true}],"speech":[{"t":"Ohio Constitution free-speech clause: not established here","d":"The Ohio Constitution has its own speech, press, assembly, and petition guarantees, but the collected texts include neither the Constitution nor case law. Whether Ohio courts read them more broadly than the First Amendment was not checked.","c":"Ohio Const. art. I, §§ 3, 11 (not in collected texts)","v":false},{"t":"UPEPA protects speech and press on matters of public concern","d":"R.C. Chapter 2747 expressly protects the exercise of freedom of speech and of the press, assembly, petition, and association under the U.S. and Ohio Constitutions on matters of public concern, and must be construed broadly to protect those rights.","c":"R.C. 2747.01(B)(3); R.C. 2747.06(B)","v":true}],"other":[{"t":"Final, appealable orders are defined by statute","d":"Appellate jurisdiction depends on R.C. 2505.02(B)’s list of final orders, such as orders affecting a substantial right that determine the action, provisional-remedy orders meeting the statute’s tests, and denials of UPEPA motions. In multi-claim or multi-party cases, a ruling on fewer than all claims also needs a Civ.R. 54(B) finding of ‘no just reason for delay’.","c":"R.C. 2505.02(B); Civ.R. 54(B)","v":true},{"t":"Magistrates do much of the trial-court work","d":"Courts may refer motions and non-jury trials to magistrates. Objections to a magistrate’s decision are due within 14 days, and failing to object to a finding or conclusion forfeits all but plain-error review on appeal.","c":"Civ.R. 53(D)(3)(b)(i), (iv)","v":true}]},"appeals":{"asof":"November 10, 2026","src":"Ohio Rules of Appellate Procedure (Supreme Court of Ohio compilation, amendments through July 1, 2026); Rules of Practice of the Supreme Court of Ohio (effective November 1, 2026); Ohio Rules of Civil Procedure (through July 1, 2026), Rules 50, 53, 54(B), 58, 59, 62; R.C. 2505.02 and R.C. 2747.05 (codes.ohio.gov). Official texts from supremecourt.ohio.gov and codes.ohio.gov; summaries are Consi’s. Each court of appeals also has local rules that were not collected.","intro":{"t":"Appeals in Ohio: 30 days to the district court of appeals, then 45 days to the Supreme Court","d":"Civil appeals of right go from the trial court (usually the court of common pleas) to one of Ohio’s district courts of appeals, which sit in three-judge panels. The appeal must be from a final order under R.C. 2505.02, with a Civ.R. 54(B) ‘no just reason for delay’ finding when fewer than all claims or parties are resolved. The notice of appeal is filed in the trial court within 30 days, and that deadline cannot be extended.\nFurther review is mostly discretionary. The Supreme Court of Ohio takes jurisdictional appeals only when it agrees the case raises a substantial constitutional question or is of public or great general interest. It also hears certified conflicts between districts. Supreme Court deadlines are mandatory, and its Clerk refuses late filings."},"groups":[["Getting into the court of appeals",[{"t":"Notice of appeal: in the trial court, with the judgment attached","d":"File the notice of appeal with the clerk of the trial court, attaching a time-stamped copy of the judgment or order appealed. The notice names each appellant, designates the judgment or order (or part) appealed, and names the court of appeals. Only a late notice is fatal; other defects allow the court to act as it considers appropriate, including dismissal. Courts may require a docketing statement by local rule.","c":"App.R. 3(A), (D)"},{"t":"Time: 30 days from entry, or from late clerk service","d":"Appeal within 30 days of entry of a final order, or within 30 days after a non-final order becomes final. If the clerk did not serve notice of the judgment within the 3 days required by Civ.R. 58(B), the 30 days run from when service is actually completed. ‘Entry’ means entry under Civ.R. 58(A). A notice filed after the decision is announced but before entry is treated as filed immediately after entry.","c":"App.R. 4(A), (C), (D); Civ.R. 58(B)"},{"t":"Tolling post-judgment motions","d":"The appeal clock starts for all parties when the trial court resolves the last of these timely filings: a Civ.R. 50(B) motion, a Civ.R. 59 new-trial motion, objections to a magistrate’s decision, a request for findings of fact and conclusions of law, a motion for attorney fees, or a motion for prejudgment interest. If a notice of appeal is filed while one is pending, the court of appeals remands and stays the appeal. Afterward, a party amends the notice (leave is granted if sought within 30 days) or files a new notice. Civ.R. 60(B) motions are not on the list.","c":"App.R. 4(B)(2)"},{"t":"Cross-appeals and second appeals","d":"An appellee who wants to change the judgment, or an interlocutory ruling that would merge into it if reversed, must file a notice of cross-appeal. An appellee who only defends the judgment on other grounds needs no cross-appeal or cross-assignment of error. Any other party may appeal within the original period or within 10 days after the first notice, whichever is later. Cross-appeals receive their own case number.","c":"App.R. 3(C), (F); App.R. 4(B)(1)"},{"t":"Final orders and partial judgments","d":"Whether an order is appealable is governed by R.C. 2505.02(B). A ruling on fewer than all claims or parties is final only with a Civ.R. 54(B) express determination that there is no just reason for delay. If an appeal is allowed from a partial order not entered under Civ.R. 54(B), a party may appeal within 30 days of that order or of the order disposing of the remaining claims.","c":"R.C. 2505.02(B); Civ.R. 54(B); App.R. 4(B)(5)"},{"t":"Interlocutory appeals in civil cases","d":"There is no general discretionary interlocutory appeal in civil cases; immediate review depends on the order fitting R.C. 2505.02(B), such as certain provisional-remedy orders. One statutory example: denial of a UPEPA (anti-SLAPP) motion for expedited relief is a final order, appealable within 30 days of entry. During that appeal, all proceedings among all parties are stayed.","c":"R.C. 2505.02(B)(4), (B)(9); R.C. 2747.05(C); R.C. 2747.03(B)"},{"t":"Stays and supersedeas bonds","d":"Seek a stay or bond approval in the trial court first. A motion to the court of appeals must show that trial-court relief was impracticable or was denied by journal entry, and it is ordinarily decided by at least two judges. An appellant gets a stay as of right by posting an adequate supersedeas bond approved by the trial court; the State and its subdivisions need no bond. Ohio has no automatic post-judgment stay.","c":"App.R. 7(A)-(B); Civ.R. 62"}]],["Record and briefing in the court of appeals",[{"t":"Transcript: order it and file the App.R. 9(B)(5) statement with the notice","d":"The appellant must order the transcript in writing and file the transcript order with the trial clerk. Unless the whole transcript will be included, the appellant files with the notice of appeal a statement listing the assignments of error and the parts to be transcribed. The appellee may designate more parts within 10 days. Without a usable recording, use a statement of the evidence (App.R. 9(C)) or an agreed statement (App.R. 9(D)). Errors in a magistrate’s factual findings require the transcript or affidavit that was filed in the trial court.","c":"App.R. 9(B)(3)-(5), 9(C), 9(D)"},{"t":"Record due in 40 days (20 on the accelerated calendar)","d":"The record is transmitted when complete, or 40 days after the last notice of appeal (20 days in accelerated cases), unless extended. Extensions must be sought within the original or an extended period. If the appellant fails to arrange timely transmission, the appeal may be dismissed after notice, with 10 days to respond.","c":"App.R. 10(A)-(C); App.R. 11(C)"},{"t":"Brief schedule: 20/20/10 days","d":"The appellant’s brief is due 20 days after the clerk mails notice that the record was filed; the appellee’s brief 20 days after service of that brief; and the reply 10 days after service of the appellee’s brief. Three days are added after service by mail or commercial carrier, but not to court-ordered dates. Extensions require a motion showing good cause. An appellant who misses the deadline risks dismissal; an appellee who misses it may lose oral argument, and the court may accept the appellant’s facts.","c":"App.R. 18(A), (C); App.R. 14(B)-(C)"},{"t":"Accelerated calendar: 15/15, no reply","d":"In districts with an accelerated calendar, cases needing little or no transcript (for example, summary judgment), agreed-statement cases, and cases designated by local rule go on it. Briefs are due 15 and 15 days, and replies only if ordered. A motion to move the case to the regular calendar is due within 7 days of assignment; a unique issue of substantial precedential value ordinarily counts as good cause.","c":"App.R. 3(H)(1); App.R. 11.1(C)"},{"t":"Brief contents and word limits","d":"Required parts: tables of contents and authorities, assignments of error with record references, issues presented, statement of the case, statement of facts with record citations, argument by assignment of error, and conclusion. An assignment not identified in the record or not separately argued may be disregarded.\nLimits: 9,000 words for initial and answer briefs and 4,500 for replies, excluding covers, tables, certificates, signature blocks, and appendices. Thirty pages (15 for a reply) in 12-point type are presumed compliant. Local rules may change the limits, and signing certifies compliance. Use 12-point type, double spaced. Amicus briefs need consent of all parties or leave.","c":"App.R. 12(A)(2); App.R. 16(A)-(C); App.R. 17; App.R. 19(A)"},{"t":"Motions and oral argument","d":"Responses to motions are due within 10 days after service, and replies within 7. Procedural motions may be decided at once. Oral argument is scheduled in all cases unless a local rule requires a request, made by printing ‘ORAL ARGUMENT REQUESTED’ on the brief cover. Each side gets 15 minutes. The panel’s names are disclosed at least 14 days before argument or submission. Additional authorities must be given in writing at least 5 days before argument.","c":"App.R. 15(A)-(B); App.R. 21(A)-(C), (I)"},{"t":"Filing and e-filing","d":"File with the clerk of the court of appeals in the county where the case originated; papers sent anywhere else are not filed. Paper filings are timely only if received, except that briefs count as filed when mailed by first-class mail or given to a commercial carrier for delivery within 3 days. Electronic filing is available only where adopted by local rule. Service may be by e-mail to an address listed on a prior filing, or through the clerk’s e-filing system if it serves documents.","c":"App.R. 13(A)-(B), (D)"},{"t":"Frivolous appeals and costs","d":"The court may order an appellant in a frivolous appeal to pay the appellee’s reasonable expenses, including attorney fees. Costs (record, transcript, filing fee, not brief printing) generally fall on the appellant if the judgment is affirmed or the appeal dismissed, and on the appellee if it is reversed.","c":"App.R. 23; App.R. 24"}]],["After the court of appeals decides",[{"t":"Reconsideration and en banc: 15 days, 15 pages","d":"An application for reconsideration and/or en banc consideration is due 15 days after the judgment is filed; seeking both requires one document unless a local rule says otherwise. Limit: 15 pages; responses are due in 15 days. It must explain in detail each point of law or fact the court overlooked or misconstrued; it is not a vehicle to reargue. En banc review is granted only for conflicting decisions within the district on a dispositive issue. Extensions are granted only for the most compelling reasons, and an untimely application without a motion to extend will not be considered.","c":"App.R. 26(A)"},{"t":"Motion to certify a conflict: 10 days","d":"Move in the court of appeals to certify a conflict with another district within 10 days after the clerk mails the judgment and notes the mailing on the docket. Oppositions are due in 10 days and replies in 7. The court rules within 60 days. The motion does not extend the time to appeal to the Supreme Court, and the time to file it cannot be extended.","c":"App.R. 25; App.R. 14(B)"},{"t":"Judgment entry","d":"A court of appeals judgment takes effect only when the clerk journalizes it. For a Supreme Court appeal, the date the judgment entry is filed with the clerk is the date of entry.","c":"App.R. 22; S.Ct.Prac.R. 7.01(A)(1)(a)(i)"}]],["Supreme Court of Ohio",[{"t":"Jurisdictional (discretionary) appeal: 45 days, memorandum filed with the notice","d":"File the notice of appeal and a memorandum in support of jurisdiction in the Supreme Court within 45 days of the court of appeals’ entry. The deadline is mandatory and the Clerk refuses late filings. Only if a stay of the court of appeals’ judgment is sought may the notice and stay motion come first, with the memorandum still due by day 45.\nA timely App.R. 26(A) reconsideration or en banc application restarts the 45 days from the ruling on it. Other parties may appeal or cross-appeal within the 45 days or 10 days after the first notice, whichever is later. The filing fee is $100.","c":"S.Ct.Prac.R. 3.04; 5.02(A); 7.01(A)(1)-(3), (5)-(6)"},{"t":"Jurisdictional memoranda: 15 pages, no reply","d":"The memorandum in support may not exceed 15 numbered pages. It must explain why the case involves a substantial constitutional question or is of public or great general interest, and it attaches the date-stamped court of appeals opinion and judgment entry. The appellee’s response is due 30 days later (20 in parental-rights, adoption, and bail cases), also capped at 15 pages. Supplemental and reply memoranda are prohibited. The Court may accept, decline, or accept and decide summarily.","c":"S.Ct.Prac.R. 7.02(B)-(D); 7.03(A)-(B); 7.04; 7.08(B)"},{"t":"Appeals of right and certified conflicts","d":"Appeals of right (for example, in cases that began in the court of appeals) need a notice within 45 days of entry. In a certified-conflict case, the court of appeals’ certification order must be filed in the Supreme Court within 45 days after that order. A party pursuing both a certified conflict and a jurisdictional appeal must tell the Supreme Court about the pending conflict motion.","c":"S.Ct.Prac.R. 6.01(A); 8.01(D); 7.07"},{"t":"Merit briefs: 40/30/20 days; 50-page limit","d":"If jurisdiction is accepted, the appellant’s merit brief is due 40 days after the Clerk files the record (20 days in parental-rights, adoption, and bail cases); the appellee’s brief 30 days after the appellant’s brief (20); and the reply 20 days after the appellee’s brief (15 in parental-rights and adoption cases). Limits: 50 numbered pages for principal briefs and 20 for the reply, excluding tables and appendix; death-penalty cases are exempt. The appellee may not add propositions of law.","c":"S.Ct.Prac.R. 16.02(A), (C); 16.03(A)-(C); 16.04(A)-(B)"},{"t":"Extensions: one stipulated 20-day extension, or one 10-day court extension","d":"The Supreme Court generally does not extend deadlines, and the Clerk refuses extension requests. Exception: for merit briefs (and responses to original-action complaints), each party may get either one stipulated extension of up to 20 days, filed before the original deadline, or, where no stipulation can be obtained, one court-granted extension of up to 10 days for good cause. An extension for one party covers its whole side. Motions to waive the filing deadlines are prohibited.","c":"S.Ct.Prac.R. 3.02(B); 3.03(B)"},{"t":"Reconsideration in the Supreme Court: 10 days","d":"A motion for reconsideration is due 10 days after the judgment entry or order is filed. It is allowed only for refusal to accept jurisdiction, sua sponte dismissal, grant of a motion to dismiss, or a merits decision, and it may not reargue the case. Oppositions are due in 10 days. Amici may not move but may file supporting memoranda.","c":"S.Ct.Prac.R. 18.02"},{"t":"E-filing and timing in the Supreme Court","d":"The E-Filing Portal is optional; filings are PDFs, and anything received after 11:59:59 p.m. Columbus time is filed the next business day. Paper received after 5:00 p.m. is reviewed the next business day but is timely if received by the due date. Deadlines falling on a weekend or holiday roll to 11:59:59 p.m. the next business day. For periods under 7 days, weekends and holidays are counted (unlike Civ.R. 6(A)). Out-of-state counsel must be admitted pro hac vice (S.Ct.Prac.R. 2.02) to file beyond the papers that perfect an appeal.","c":"S.Ct.Prac.R. 2.01(A); 3.02(A)(2)-(3); 3.03(A)"}]],["Deadlines",[{"t":"Notice of appeal to court of appeals (civil)","d":"30 days from entry of the final judgment or order (or clerk’s actual service if not made within Civ.R. 58(B)’s 3 days).\nFiled in the trial court. The court of appeals cannot extend it (App.R. 14(B)).","c":"App.R. 4(A)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (entry of the final judgment or order)","note":"Filed in the trial court. The court of appeals cannot extend it (App.R. 14(B))."}},{"t":"Notice of appeal by another party / cross-appeal","d":"10 days from filing of the first timely notice of appeal (or the original 30 days, if later).\nNeeded only if the appellee seeks to change the judgment.","c":"App.R. 4(B)(1); App.R. 3(C)","due":{"n":10,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the first timely notice of appeal)","note":"Needed only if the appellee seeks to change the judgment."}},{"t":"Restarted appeal period after tolling motions","d":"30 days from entry of the order resolving the last timely Civ.R. 50(B), 59, magistrate-objection, findings, attorney-fee, or prejudgment-interest filing.\nCiv.R. 60(B) motions do not toll.","c":"App.R. 4(B)(2)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (entry of the order resolving the last timely Civ.R. 50(B), 59, magistrate-objection, findings, attorney-fee, or prejudgment-interest filing)","note":"Civ.R. 60(B) motions do not toll."}},{"t":"Civ.R. 50(B) JNOV / Civ.R. 59 new-trial motion","d":"28 days from entry of judgment (or the clerk’s actual service if late).\nCannot be extended (Civ.R. 6(B)(2)(e)).","c":"Civ.R. 50(B); Civ.R. 59(B)","due":{"n":28,"from":"the trigger date (entry of judgment)","note":"Cannot be extended (Civ.R. 6(B)(2)(e))."}},{"t":"Objections to magistrate’s decision","d":"14 days from filing of the magistrate’s decision.\nFailure to object forfeits all but plain-error review.","c":"Civ.R. 53(D)(3)(b)(i)","due":{"n":14,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the magistrate’s decision)","note":"Failure to object forfeits all but plain-error review."}},{"t":"App.R. 9(B)(5) transcript statement / docketing statement","d":"0 days from filed with the notice of appeal.\nThe record is deemed complete if not filed within 10 days of the notice (App.R. 10(B)(7)).","c":"App.R. 9(B)(5); App.R. 3(H)","due":{"n":0,"from":"the trigger date (filed with the notice of appeal)","note":"The record is deemed complete if not filed within 10 days of the notice (App.R. 10(B)(7))."}},{"t":"Appellee’s designation of additional transcript","d":"10 days from service of the appellant’s App.R. 9(B)(5) statement.","c":"App.R. 9(B)(5)","due":{"n":10,"from":"the trigger date (service of the appellant’s App.R. 9(B)(5) statement)","note":""}},{"t":"Transmission of record","d":"40 days from filing of the last notice of appeal.\n20 days on the accelerated calendar. Extensions must be requested before the period expires.","c":"App.R. 10(A)","due":{"n":40,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the last notice of appeal)","note":"20 days on the accelerated calendar. Extensions must be requested before the period expires."}},{"t":"Appellant’s brief","d":"20 days from clerk’s mailing of notice that the record was filed.\n15 days on the accelerated calendar (App.R. 11.1(C)).","c":"App.R. 18(A)","due":{"n":20,"from":"the trigger date (clerk’s mailing of notice that the record was filed)","note":"15 days on the accelerated calendar (App.R. 11.1(C))."}},{"t":"Appellee’s brief","d":"20 days from service of the appellant’s brief.\n15 days on the accelerated calendar.","c":"App.R. 18(A)","due":{"n":20,"from":"the trigger date (service of the appellant’s brief)","note":"15 days on the accelerated calendar."}},{"t":"Reply brief","d":"10 days from service of the appellee’s brief.\nNot allowed on the accelerated calendar unless ordered.","c":"App.R. 18(A)","due":{"n":10,"from":"the trigger date (service of the appellee’s brief)","note":"Not allowed on the accelerated calendar unless ordered."}},{"t":"Response to motion in court of appeals","d":"10 days from service of the motion.\nReply in 7 days.","c":"App.R. 15(A)","due":{"n":10,"from":"the trigger date (service of the motion)","note":"Reply in 7 days."}},{"t":"Application for reconsideration / en banc","d":"15 days from filing of the court of appeals’ judgment.\n15-page limit; response in 15 days.","c":"App.R. 26(A)(1)(a), (A)(2)(c)","due":{"n":15,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the court of appeals’ judgment)","note":"15-page limit; response in 15 days."}},{"t":"Motion to certify a conflict","d":"10 days from clerk’s mailing of the judgment and docket notation.\nDoes not extend the 45-day Supreme Court deadline and cannot itself be extended.","c":"App.R. 25(A)","due":{"n":10,"from":"the trigger date (clerk’s mailing of the judgment and docket notation)","note":"Does not extend the 45-day Supreme Court deadline and cannot itself be extended."}},{"t":"Notice of appeal + memorandum in support of jurisdiction to Supreme Court","d":"45 days from filing of the court of appeals’ judgment entry.\nMandatory; runs from the ruling on a timely App.R. 26(A) application. 15-page memorandum.","c":"S.Ct.Prac.R. 7.01(A)(1)","due":{"n":45,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the court of appeals’ judgment entry)","note":"Mandatory; runs from the ruling on a timely App.R. 26(A) application. 15-page memorandum."}},{"t":"Memorandum in response to jurisdiction","d":"30 days from filing of the memorandum in support.\n20 days in parental-rights, adoption, and bail cases.","c":"S.Ct.Prac.R. 7.03(A)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the memorandum in support)","note":"20 days in parental-rights, adoption, and bail cases."}},{"t":"Certified-conflict filing in Supreme Court","d":"45 days from date of the court of appeals’ order certifying the conflict.","c":"S.Ct.Prac.R. 8.01(D)","due":{"n":45,"from":"the trigger date (date of the court of appeals’ order certifying the conflict)","note":""}},{"t":"Supreme Court merit brief (appellant)","d":"40 days from clerk’s filing of the record.\nAppellee 30 days later; reply 20 days later. 50/50/20-page limits.","c":"S.Ct.Prac.R. 16.02(A)","due":{"n":40,"from":"the trigger date (clerk’s filing of the record)","note":"Appellee 30 days later; reply 20 days later. 50/50/20-page limits."}},{"t":"Supreme Court motion for reconsideration","d":"10 days from filing of the judgment entry or order.","c":"S.Ct.Prac.R. 18.02(A)(1)","due":{"n":10,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the judgment entry or order)","note":""}},{"t":"Pro hac vice motion in Supreme Court","d":"30 days from filing of the appeal (or at least 30 days before oral argument).","c":"S.Ct.Prac.R. 2.02(B)(3)-(4)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the appeal)","note":""}},{"t":"UPEPA (anti-SLAPP) denial appeal","d":"30 days from entry of the order denying the motion for expedited relief.\nInterlocutory appeal of right; all proceedings stayed during the appeal.","c":"R.C. 2747.05(C)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (entry of the order denying the motion for expedited relief)","note":"Interlocutory appeal of right; all proceedings stayed during the appeal."}}]]],"dl":[["Notice of appeal to court of appeals (civil)",30,"Entry of the final judgment or order (or clerk’s actual service if not made within Civ.R. 58(B)’s 3 days)","App.R. 4(A)","Filed in the trial court. The court of appeals cannot extend it (App.R. 14(B))."],["Notice of appeal by another party / cross-appeal",10,"Filing of the first timely notice of appeal (or the original 30 days, if later)","App.R. 4(B)(1); App.R. 3(C)","Needed only if the appellee seeks to change the judgment."],["Restarted appeal period after tolling motions",30,"Entry of the order resolving the last timely Civ.R. 50(B), 59, magistrate-objection, findings, attorney-fee, or prejudgment-interest filing","App.R. 4(B)(2)","Civ.R. 60(B) motions do not toll."],["Civ.R. 50(B) JNOV / Civ.R. 59 new-trial motion",28,"Entry of judgment (or the clerk’s actual service if late)","Civ.R. 50(B); Civ.R. 59(B)","Cannot be extended (Civ.R. 6(B)(2)(e))."],["Objections to magistrate’s decision",14,"Filing of the magistrate’s decision","Civ.R. 53(D)(3)(b)(i)","Failure to object forfeits all but plain-error review."],["App.R. 9(B)(5) transcript statement / docketing statement",0,"Filed with the notice of appeal","App.R. 9(B)(5); App.R. 3(H)","The record is deemed complete if not filed within 10 days of the notice (App.R. 10(B)(7))."],["Appellee’s designation of additional transcript",10,"Service of the appellant’s App.R. 9(B)(5) statement","App.R. 9(B)(5)",""],["Transmission of record",40,"Filing of the last notice of appeal","App.R. 10(A)","20 days on the accelerated calendar. Extensions must be requested before the period expires."],["Appellant’s brief",20,"Clerk’s mailing of notice that the record was filed","App.R. 18(A)","15 days on the accelerated calendar (App.R. 11.1(C))."],["Appellee’s brief",20,"Service of the appellant’s brief","App.R. 18(A)","15 days on the accelerated calendar."],["Reply brief",10,"Service of the appellee’s brief","App.R. 18(A)","Not allowed on the accelerated calendar unless ordered."],["Response to motion in court of appeals",10,"Service of the motion","App.R. 15(A)","Reply in 7 days."],["Application for reconsideration / en banc",15,"Filing of the court of appeals’ judgment","App.R. 26(A)(1)(a), (A)(2)(c)","15-page limit; response in 15 days."],["Motion to certify a conflict",10,"Clerk’s mailing of the judgment and docket notation","App.R. 25(A)","Does not extend the 45-day Supreme Court deadline and cannot itself be extended."],["Notice of appeal + memorandum in support of jurisdiction to Supreme Court",45,"Filing of the court of appeals’ judgment entry","S.Ct.Prac.R. 7.01(A)(1)","Mandatory; runs from the ruling on a timely App.R. 26(A) application. 15-page memorandum."],["Memorandum in response to jurisdiction",30,"Filing of the memorandum in support","S.Ct.Prac.R. 7.03(A)","20 days in parental-rights, adoption, and bail cases."],["Certified-conflict filing in Supreme Court",45,"Date of the court of appeals’ order certifying the conflict","S.Ct.Prac.R. 8.01(D)",""],["Supreme Court merit brief (appellant)",40,"Clerk’s filing of the record","S.Ct.Prac.R. 16.02(A)","Appellee 30 days later; reply 20 days later. 50/50/20-page limits."],["Supreme Court motion for reconsideration",10,"Filing of the judgment entry or order","S.Ct.Prac.R. 18.02(A)(1)",""],["Pro hac vice motion in Supreme Court",30,"Filing of the appeal (or at least 30 days before oral argument)","S.Ct.Prac.R. 2.02(B)(3)-(4)",""],["UPEPA (anti-SLAPP) denial appeal",30,"Entry of the order denying the motion for expedited relief","R.C. 2747.05(C)","Interlocutory appeal of right; all proceedings stayed during the appeal."]],"courts":[],"links":[["Official site","https://www.supremecourt.ohio.gov/"]]},"rulesName":"Ohio Rules of Civil Procedure","rulesSrc":"https://www.supremecourt.ohio.gov/docs/LegalResources/Rules/civil/CivilProcedure.pdf","rulesAsof":"as checked November 10, 2026","rulesNote":"Each entry is our summary of the rule, not the rule itself. Tap Read the official text before you rely on it.","rules":[{"n":"1","t":"Scope of rules; construction; exceptions","s":"The Civil Rules govern civil cases in all Ohio courts and are construed to secure just results without delay or unnecessary expense, except under Rule 73 (probate) and Rule 75 (domestic relations) and except where clearly inapplicable to appeals, appropriation of property, forcible entry and detainer, small claims under R.C. Chapter 1925, uniform reciprocal support actions, commitment of the mentally ill, adoptions under R.C. Chapter 3107, and other special statutory proceedings. Any option in the rules to appear by live two-way video does not limit a court’s power to order a party, attorney, or witness to appear physically.","u":"https://www.supremecourt.ohio.gov/docs/LegalResources/Rules/civil/CivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"1.1","t":"Definitions","s":"Defines “appear,” “appearance,” “in person,” and “attendance” to include physical or remote presence; “open court” includes proceedings the public can attend in person or by remote access; “personally” includes remote presence except for service of process under Civ.R. 4.1–4.5 and subpoenas under Civ.R. 45; and “remote presence” means participating by live two-way video and audio.","u":"https://www.supremecourt.ohio.gov/docs/LegalResources/Rules/civil/CivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"2","t":"One form of action","s":"There is only one form of action, the civil action.","u":"https://www.supremecourt.ohio.gov/docs/LegalResources/Rules/civil/CivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"3","t":"Commencement of action; venue","s":"An action is commenced by filing a complaint only if service is obtained on a named (or later correctly named or fictitiously named) defendant within one year of filing; unlike FRCP 3, filing alone does not commence the action. An attorney may make a limited appearance by filing a notice, and it ends on filing a notice of completion if no one objects within 10 days. The rule itself sets venue (unlike federal practice, where venue is statutory), listing proper counties such as the defendant’s residence or principal place of business, where the claim arose, where property lies, for divorce or legal separation the plaintiff’s county of residence for at least 90 days before filing, and for civil protection orders the petitioner’s county; improper venue leads to transfer (with possible costs and fees), a transferred defendant in default gets 28 days from notice to answer, and if no Ohio forum is proper the case is stayed so the plaintiff can refile in another state within 60 days or face dismissal without prejudice.","u":"https://www.supremecourt.ohio.gov/docs/LegalResources/Rules/civil/CivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"4","t":"Summons","s":"On filing, the clerk issues a summons, with the complaint attached, for each defendant, stating the time to appear and warning of default. A person at least 18 and not under disability, or the party’s attorney, may waive service in writing (an individual’s own waiver must give a mailing or e-mail address). If service is not made within six months after filing and there is no good cause, the action is dismissed without prejudice as to that defendant (unlike FRCP 4(m)’s 90 days); this limit does not apply to out-of-state or foreign service. A motion to revive a dormant judgment is served with a summons like a complaint.","u":"https://www.supremecourt.ohio.gov/docs/LegalResources/Rules/civil/CivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"4.1","t":"Methods of service","s":"In-state service is made by the clerk by certified or express mail, or by a commercial carrier requiring a signed receipt, with return receipt docketed and the serving party notified of any failure. On written request, the plaintiff may instead use personal service (handing the papers to the person) or residence service (leaving them at the person’s usual residence with someone of suitable age and discretion living there), carried out by the sheriff, a municipal court bailiff, or a court-designated process server, who must return the process with the reasons if unable to serve within 28 days (late service or late proof does not invalidate service). A court may designate a process server for up to one year at a time if the applicant certifies being at least 18, disinterested, a U.S. citizen or legal resident, holding valid ID, free of felony, violence, or dishonesty convictions in the last 10 years and not on supervision, not subject to a civil protection order, and familiar with service procedure.","u":"https://www.supremecourt.ohio.gov/docs/LegalResources/Rules/civil/CivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"4.2","t":"Who may be served","s":"Specifies whom to serve: individuals directly; persons under 16 through a guardian or the parent or caretaker they live with; incompetent persons through a guardian or institution head; prisoners directly; corporations, LLCs, partnerships, unincorporated and professional associations through a registered agent, an officer, manager, member, partner, or shareholder, or by clerk mail service to a usual place of business; and the state, counties, townships, municipalities, and other public bodies through the responsible officer or their legal officer (the attorney general, county prosecutor, township law director, or city solicitor). Address-confidentiality program participants may be served through the Secretary of State.","u":"https://www.supremecourt.ohio.gov/docs/LegalResources/Rules/civil/CivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"4.3","t":"Out-of-state service","s":"Allows service outside Ohio on nonresidents (or absent residents) whose claim-related conduct falls within the long-arm list, including transacting business or contracting to supply goods or services in Ohio, causing tortious injury in or affecting Ohio, owning or using Ohio real property, insuring Ohio risks, living in a marriage in Ohio as to support, custody, and property obligations, and criminal acts with an element in Ohio. Service may be by clerk mail or commercial carrier as under Civ.R. 4.1, or, when the court orders, by personal service by a court-designated nonparty at least 18 years old; failure to serve within 28 days or to file proof does not invalidate service.","u":"https://www.supremecourt.ohio.gov/docs/LegalResources/Rules/civil/CivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"4.4","t":"Service by publication","s":"When a party’s residence is unknown and publication is authorized by law, the requesting party files an affidavit describing diligent efforts to find it, and notice is published in a newspaper of general circulation once a week for six successive weeks (unless law allows fewer); service is complete on the last publication, and the defendant must answer within 28 days after it. In divorce, annulment, legal separation, custody of children of unmarried parents, and post-decree matters where the requester has a poverty affidavit, and in civil protection order cases without one, service is instead by posting in the courthouse and two other designated public places (or, except in protection order cases, on the clerk’s website) for six successive weeks, plus ordinary mail to the last known address, with service complete when the posting is docketed. If the residence is known, other service methods must be used first.","u":"https://www.supremecourt.ohio.gov/docs/LegalResources/Rules/civil/CivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"4.5","t":"Service in a foreign country","s":"Service in a Hague Service Convention country must use a method the Convention allows, including Article 8 or 10 methods the country has not objected to. Elsewhere, service may be by clerk mail or carrier, publication if applicable, the foreign country’s own methods, letter rogatory, personal delivery, delivery to an entity’s officer or managing agent, signed-receipt delivery addressed by the clerk, or as the court orders; personal or court-ordered service may be made by a nonparty at least 18 designated by the court or by the foreign court. Proof may follow Civ.R. 4.1(B), foreign law, or court order, and missing the 28-day period does not invalidate service.","u":"https://www.supremecourt.ohio.gov/docs/LegalResources/Rules/civil/CivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"4.6","t":"Service limits; amendment; refused or unclaimed service","s":"Process may be served anywhere in Ohio and outside Ohio when authorized, and the court may allow amendment of process or proof of service absent material prejudice. If certified, express, or carrier service is refused (or personal or residence service is refused) or the mail is returned unclaimed, the clerk notifies the serving party, who may request ordinary mail service; service is complete when the mailing is entered of record (for unclaimed mail, unless the ordinary mail comes back undelivered), and the answer is due 28 days after the date of mailing. The attorney or serving party, not the clerk, is ultimately responsible for confirming service and instructing the clerk.","u":"https://www.supremecourt.ohio.gov/docs/LegalResources/Rules/civil/CivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"4.7","t":"Waiving service","s":"A plaintiff may mail (first-class or other reliable means) a written notice and request to waive service, with the complaint, two copies of the official waiver form, and prepaid return, giving the defendant at least 28 days to return it (60 days if outside the United States); unlike FRCP 4(d), which allows at least 30 days, and unlike federal practice, this procedure applies only in common pleas courts and not to civil protection orders or domestic relations matters. A defendant who waives has until 60 days after the request was sent to answer (90 days if sent to a foreign country), keeps all jurisdiction and venue objections, and a defendant who refuses without good cause can be charged the later service costs and the fees of any motion to collect them.","u":"https://www.supremecourt.ohio.gov/docs/LegalResources/Rules/civil/CivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"5","t":"Service and filing of later pleadings and papers","s":"Every later pleading, written motion (other than ex parte), discovery paper, notice, and similar document must be served on every party (on the attorney if represented, and on both the attorney and the party for a limited appearance), but parties in default for failure to appear need be served only with new or additional claims, by summons. Service may be by hand, leaving at the office or home, mail (complete on mailing), commercial carrier for delivery within three calendar days (complete on delivery to carrier), leaving with the clerk if no known address, or e-mail or fax to an address given under Civ.R. 11 (complete on transmission unless the sender learns it failed), and each document needs a proof of service naming the method. Papers must be filed within three days after service (unlike FRCP 5(d)’s “reasonable time”), but discovery requests and responses are not filed until used or ordered, and courts must provide for electronic filing by order or local rule.","u":"https://www.supremecourt.ohio.gov/docs/LegalResources/Rules/civil/CivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"6","t":"Time","s":"Exclude the triggering day and include the last day unless it is a weekend, legal holiday, or the office is closed; for periods under seven days, intermediate weekends and holidays are excluded (unlike FRCP 6(a), which counts every day). The court may extend time for good cause before it expires, or after on motion for excusable neglect, but not the deadlines under Civ.R. 50(B), 59(B), 59(D), and 60(B); a continuance of a trial or hearing requires a written motion endorsed by party and counsel, and the court must set a definite new date. Unless the court changes them, responses to motions are due in 14 days (28 days for summary judgment motions) and replies in 7 days; motions for a non-trial hearing must be served 14 days before it and trial motions 28 days before trial, with no reply allowed; and 3 days are added when a document is served by mail or commercial carrier (not electronic service, and not for summons).","u":"https://www.supremecourt.ohio.gov/docs/LegalResources/Rules/civil/CivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"7","t":"Pleadings and motions","s":"The only pleadings are a complaint and answer, a reply to a counterclaim, an answer to a cross-claim, and a third-party complaint and answer; the court may order a reply to an answer. Requests for orders are by motion, in writing unless made at a hearing or trial, stating the grounds with particularity and the relief sought, served under Civ.R. 5 and signed under Civ.R. 11; courts may decide motions on written submissions without oral hearing.","u":"https://www.supremecourt.ohio.gov/docs/LegalResources/Rules/civil/CivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"8","t":"General rules of pleading","s":"A claim needs a short and plain statement showing entitlement to relief and a demand for judgment, but a party seeking more than $25,000 must say so without stating the amount sought (unless the claim is on an attached instrument), and an opposing party may demand a written statement of the amount; unlike FRCP 8(a), the amount is not pleaded. Denials must fairly meet the averments, a lack of knowledge counts as a denial, unanswered averments other than damages are admitted, and listed affirmative defenses (such as statute of limitations, res judicata, release, waiver, laches, and fraud) must be pleaded. Pleadings may be alternative or inconsistent, are construed to do substantial justice, are not read to the jury, and must disclose a party’s minority or incompetency.","u":"https://www.supremecourt.ohio.gov/docs/LegalResources/Rules/civil/CivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"9","t":"Pleading special matters","s":"Capacity to sue need not be alleged, but a challenge to it must be made by specific negative averment. Fraud and mistake must be pleaded with particularity while intent and knowledge may be alleged generally; conditions precedent may be alleged generally but must be denied specifically; official documents and judgments may be pleaded simply; time and place are material; and special damages must be specifically stated.","u":"https://www.supremecourt.ohio.gov/docs/LegalResources/Rules/civil/CivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"10","t":"Form of pleadings","s":"Pleadings need a caption with the court, title, case number, and designation, and the complaint must list all parties’ names and addresses; claims and defenses go in numbered paragraphs, separate counts where helpful. A claim or defense founded on an account or written instrument must attach a copy or explain why not. A medical, dental, optometric, or chiropractic claim must include an expert affidavit of merit for each defendant needing expert proof, though on a motion filed with the complaint the court may allow up to 90 days (longer for discovery noncooperation), and a defective affidavit may be cured within up to 60 days; dismissal for lack of one is not on the merits. Papers may not exceed 8½ × 11 inches.","u":"https://www.supremecourt.ohio.gov/docs/LegalResources/Rules/civil/CivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"11","t":"Signing of pleadings, motions, and other documents","s":"Every document must be signed (by hand or electronically) by an attorney of record, with address, registration number, phone, and any fax and e-mail, or by an unrepresented party with address, phone, and any fax and e-mail for electronic service. The signature certifies the signer has read it, believes there is good ground to support it, and is not filing it for delay; an unsigned or sham document may be stricken, and a willful violation (or scandalous or indecent matter) can bring sanctions including the opponent’s expenses and attorney fees. Unlike FRCP 11, the Ohio standard is subjective good faith with a willfulness requirement, and there is no 21-day safe harbor.","u":"https://www.supremecourt.ohio.gov/docs/LegalResources/Rules/civil/CivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"12","t":"Defenses and objections","s":"Answer within 28 days after service of summons and complaint (or after publication is complete); answers to cross-claims and replies to counterclaims are also due in 28 days (unlike the federal 21 days). Defenses of lack of subject-matter or personal jurisdiction, improper venue, insufficient process or service, failure to state a claim, and failure to join a party under Civ.R. 19 or 19.1 may be raised by pre-answer motion, after which the response is due 14 days after notice of denial or after service of the amended pleading if granted; personal jurisdiction, venue, and process defenses are waived if left out of a first motion or responsive pleading, while failure to state a claim and failure to join an indispensable party can be raised through trial, and lack of subject-matter jurisdiction anytime. A motion that relies on outside matters becomes a summary judgment motion; a definite-statement order must be obeyed within 14 days; and a motion to strike is made before responding or, if no response is allowed, within 28 days after service.","u":"https://www.supremecourt.ohio.gov/docs/LegalResources/Rules/civil/CivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"13","t":"Counterclaim and cross-claim","s":"A counterclaim arising from the same transaction or occurrence as the opposing claim is compulsory unless it is already pending elsewhere or the suit was by attachment without personal jurisdiction; other counterclaims are permissive and may exceed or differ from the opposing claim. Later-maturing counterclaims may be added by supplemental pleading with leave, and omitted ones by amendment with leave; cross-claims against co-parties must arise from the same transaction or concern the same property. Unlike the federal rule, if a counterclaim, cross-claim, or third-party claim exceeds the court’s jurisdiction (e.g., in municipal court), the case is certified to the court of common pleas.","u":"https://www.supremecourt.ohio.gov/docs/LegalResources/Rules/civil/CivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"14","t":"Third-party practice","s":"A defending party may bring in a nonparty who may be liable for all or part of the claim against it, without leave if the third-party complaint is filed within 14 days after serving the original answer and otherwise with leave on notice. The third-party defendant answers under Civ.R. 12 and 13, may raise the defendant’s defenses against the plaintiff, and claims between plaintiff and third-party defendant may be asserted; unlike the federal rule, if the third-party defendant is the defendant’s employee or agent, the court must order a separate trial on any plaintiff’s motion. A plaintiff facing a counterclaim may also bring in a third party.","u":"https://www.supremecourt.ohio.gov/docs/LegalResources/Rules/civil/CivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"15","t":"Amended and supplemental pleadings","s":"A party may amend once as of right within 28 days after serving the pleading or, if a response is required, within 28 days after service of the responsive pleading or a Civ.R. 12(B), (E), or (F) motion, whichever is earlier (FRCP 15 uses 21 days); otherwise amendment needs written consent or leave, which is freely given, and responses to amended pleadings are due within the time remaining or 14 days after service, whichever is later. Pleadings may be amended to conform to the evidence even after judgment, and amendments relate back if they arise from the same conduct, and when changing a party if that party had notice and knew of the mistake within the limitations period. A defendant whose name is unknown may be sued by a description, with the summons marked “name unknown” and served personally, and supplemental pleadings about later events may be allowed on motion.","u":"https://www.supremecourt.ohio.gov/docs/LegalResources/Rules/civil/CivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"16","t":"Pretrial procedure","s":"The court may order pretrial conferences to expedite the case, control management, discourage waste, improve trial preparation, and encourage settlement. Except in Civ.R. 1(C) matters, the court must issue a scheduling order as soon as practicable and, absent good cause, within the earlier of 90 days after any defendant is served or 60 days after any defendant responds; it may set deadlines for joinder, amendment, discovery, and motions, address ESI and initial-disclosure timing, require a conference before discovery motions, and may be modified only for good cause with the court’s consent. Conferences cover settlement, issues, expert numbers, medical records, ESI, privilege agreements, Sup.R. 5.21–5.22 disposition deadlines, and trial dates; a final pretrial order may be modified only to prevent manifest injustice.","u":"https://www.supremecourt.ohio.gov/docs/LegalResources/Rules/civil/CivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"17","t":"Parties; capacity","s":"Actions must be prosecuted by the real party in interest, though representatives such as executors, guardians, and trustees may sue in their own names, and no dismissal for that defect is allowed until there is a reasonable time to ratify, join, or substitute the real party. A minor or incompetent person may sue or defend through a guardian or similar representative, or otherwise through a next friend or guardian ad litem, and the court must appoint a guardian ad litem or otherwise protect one who is unrepresented.","u":"https://www.supremecourt.ohio.gov/docs/LegalResources/Rules/civil/CivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"18","t":"Joinder of claims and remedies","s":"A party with a claim may join as many claims, legal or equitable, as it has against an opposing party. Claims that would ordinarily wait for another to be resolved may be joined, such as a money claim together with a claim to set aside a fraudulent conveyance, with relief granted according to the parties’ substantive rights.","u":"https://www.supremecourt.ohio.gov/docs/LegalResources/Rules/civil/CivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"19","t":"Joinder of persons needed for just adjudication","s":"A person subject to service must be joined if complete relief is impossible without them, if their absence would impair their interest or expose parties to inconsistent obligations, or (unlike FRCP 19) if they are an assignor, assignee, subrogor, or subrogee; the defense must be timely raised under Civ.R. 12(B)(7) or is waived. If joinder is not feasible, the court decides in equity and good conscience whether to proceed or dismiss, weighing prejudice, ways to lessen it, adequacy of a judgment, and the plaintiff’s alternative remedy; pleadings must name unjoined needed persons, and if joinder exceeds the court’s jurisdiction the case is certified to common pleas.","u":"https://www.supremecourt.ohio.gov/docs/LegalResources/Rules/civil/CivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"19.1","t":"Compulsory joinder","s":"An Ohio-specific rule with no federal counterpart: unless good cause is shown, persons with claims arising from the same wrongful act must be joined, including a decedent’s survival claim and the wrongful-death claim; a spouse’s injury and the other spouse’s consortium or expense claim; a minor’s injury and the parent’s or guardian’s claim; an employee’s or agent’s injury and the employer’s or principal’s property-damage claim; and a parent’s injury and an adult emancipated child’s parental-consortium claim. The defense is raised under Civ.R. 12(B)(7) or waived, pleadings must name unjoined persons and explain why, and if joinder exceeds the court’s jurisdiction the case is certified to common pleas.","u":"https://www.supremecourt.ohio.gov/docs/LegalResources/Rules/civil/CivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"20","t":"Permissive joinder of parties","s":"Plaintiffs may join, and defendants may be joined, when the claims arise from the same transaction, occurrence, or series and share a common question of law or fact; not every party need be interested in all relief, and judgment may be given according to each party’s rights or liabilities. The court may order separate trials or other protective orders to prevent prejudice, delay, or expense.","u":"https://www.supremecourt.ohio.gov/docs/LegalResources/Rules/civil/CivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"21","t":"Misjoinder and nonjoinder of parties","s":"Misjoinder is not a ground for dismissal; the court may add or drop parties on motion or on its own at any stage on just terms and may sever any claim to proceed separately.","u":"https://www.supremecourt.ohio.gov/docs/LegalResources/Rules/civil/CivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"22","t":"Interpleader","s":"A plaintiff exposed to double or multiple liability may join the competing claimants as defendants and require them to interplead, even if their claims have different origins or the plaintiff denies liability, and a defendant may interplead by cross-claim or counterclaim. With notice and leave of court, a party may deposit the disputed money or property with the court for safekeeping or disposition.","u":"https://www.supremecourt.ohio.gov/docs/LegalResources/Rules/civil/CivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"23","t":"Class actions","s":"A class action requires numerosity, common questions, typical claims, and adequate representation, plus one of three types: risk of inconsistent or impairing individual adjudications, generally applicable conduct warranting class-wide injunctive or declaratory relief, or predominance of common questions and superiority (requiring the best practicable notice, including individual notice, with a right to opt out). The court decides certification at an early practicable time, defines the class, appoints class counsel (which Ohio places in division (F), with fees in (G)), and may use issue classes and subclasses; settlements, dismissals, or compromises of a certified class require notice, a hearing, and a finding that they are fair, reasonable, and adequate. Unlike FRCP 23, class members’ claims are aggregated to determine the court’s jurisdiction.","u":"https://www.supremecourt.ohio.gov/docs/LegalResources/Rules/civil/CivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"23.1","t":"Derivative actions by shareholders","s":"A shareholder suing to enforce a corporate right must file a verified complaint alleging ownership at the time of the challenged transaction (or by later operation of law) and describing with particularity the demand made on directors and, if necessary, shareholders, or why no demand was made. The plaintiff must fairly and adequately represent similarly situated shareholders, and the case cannot be dismissed or settled without court approval and notice to shareholders as the court directs.","u":"https://www.supremecourt.ohio.gov/docs/LegalResources/Rules/civil/CivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"24","t":"Intervention","s":"On timely application, intervention is allowed as of right when an Ohio statute gives an unconditional right or the applicant has an interest that may be impaired and is not adequately represented, and permissively when a statute gives a conditional right, the applicant shares a common question, or a government agency administers a statute or order at issue, considering undue delay or prejudice. The motion must state the grounds, be served under Civ.R. 5, and be accompanied by a pleading setting out the claim or defense.","u":"https://www.supremecourt.ohio.gov/docs/LegalResources/Rules/civil/CivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"25","t":"Substitution of parties","s":"If a party dies and the claim survives, a motion to substitute must be made within 90 days after the death is suggested on the record, or the action is dismissed as to that party; the motion is served on parties under Civ.R. 5 and on nonparties like a summons. Unlike FRCP 25, the deceased or incompetent party’s attorney must suggest the death or incompetency on the record within 14 days after learning of it. Actions continue by or against a representative for incompetency, and by the original party after a transfer of interest unless the court orders substitution; a public officer’s successor is automatically substituted.","u":"https://www.supremecourt.ohio.gov/docs/LegalResources/Rules/civil/CivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"26","t":"General provisions governing discovery","s":"Unlike FRCP 26, the rule opens with a policy protecting attorneys’ trial preparation and preventing one side from exploiting the other’s work; discovery covers any nonprivileged matter relevant to any claim or defense and proportional to the needs of the case, with no limit on frequency unless the court orders one. Initial disclosures (witnesses, documents, damages computation, insurance) are due by the first pretrial or case management conference, or within 30 days for later-joined parties, with listed exemptions (unlike FRCP 26(a)’s 14 days after the planning conference). Expert reports stating all opinions, their bases, and compensation must be exchanged on the court’s schedule (burdened party first) and no later than 30 days before trial absent good cause, an expert may not testify beyond the report, treating healthcare providers may testify from their records instead, and experts may be deposed only after the report exchange; drafts and most attorney-expert communications are protected. Work product is discoverable only on good cause, privilege logs and clawback procedures apply, protective orders require a good-faith effort to resolve first, and parties must confer at least 21 days before the scheduling conference and file a discovery plan within 14 days after conferring.","u":"https://www.supremecourt.ohio.gov/docs/LegalResources/Rules/civil/CivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"27","t":"Depositions before action or pending appeal","s":"A person expecting to be a party but unable yet to bring or defend the action may file a verified petition in the common pleas court of the county where any expected adverse party resides to perpetuate testimony, serving notice like a summons at least 28 days before the hearing (unlike FRCP 27’s 21 days) unless the court shortens it for extraordinary circumstances; the court appoints an attorney to cross-examine for adverse parties who cannot get actual notice. While an appeal is pending, a party may move in the trial court for leave to take depositions to perpetuate testimony. Such depositions are filed with the court, paid for by the party taking them unless ordered otherwise, and usable in later actions under Civ.R. 32(A), generally only if the deponent is unavailable.","u":"https://www.supremecourt.ohio.gov/docs/LegalResources/Rules/civil/CivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"28","t":"Persons before whom depositions may be taken","s":"In Ohio, depositions may be taken before anyone authorized to give oaths under Ohio law, a court appointee, or a person stipulated by all parties; outside Ohio, before a local oath officer, an appointee, a stipulated person, or a U.S. consular officer abroad, and out-of-state witnesses may be deposed remotely before an Ohio oath officer. Unless the parties agree, the officer may not be a relative, employee, or attorney of a party or of a party’s attorney, or financially interested. Unique to Ohio, blanket contracts for court reporting across two or more cases at a fixed rate are prohibited, though case-by-case fee negotiation equal to all parties is allowed.","u":"https://www.supremecourt.ohio.gov/docs/LegalResources/Rules/civil/CivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"29","t":"Stipulations regarding discovery procedure","s":"Unless the court orders otherwise, the parties may agree in writing that depositions be taken before any person, at any time or place, on any notice, and in any manner, and may modify the procedures for other discovery methods.","u":"https://www.supremecourt.ohio.gov/docs/LegalResources/Rules/civil/CivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"30","t":"Depositions upon oral examination","s":"After the action begins, any party may depose any person on reasonable written notice to all parties (a prisoner only with leave), compelling a nonparty by subpoena; unlike FRCP 30, there is no 10-deposition cap or leave requirement for repeat depositions in the rule text. A deposition is limited to one day of seven hours unless stipulated or ordered; it may be recorded non-stenographically if the notice says so, taken by phone or remote presence by stipulation or order, and an organization must designate witnesses after a good-faith conference on the topics. Objections must be concise and nonsuggestive, and instructions not to answer are allowed only to preserve privilege, enforce a court limit, or move to terminate for bad faith or harassment. The witness has 30 days after submission of the transcript to review and sign (7 days if taken within 30 days of trial or hearing, or a court-set deadline if trial is under 7 days away), and the noticing party may owe expenses if it fails to attend or to subpoena the witness.","u":"https://www.supremecourt.ohio.gov/docs/LegalResources/Rules/civil/CivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"31","t":"Depositions upon written questions","s":"Any party may depose any person on written questions served with a notice naming the witness and officer, and organizations may be deposed as under Civ.R. 30(B)(5). Cross-questions are due within 21 days after the notice and questions are served, redirect questions within 14 days after cross-questions, and recross questions within 14 days after redirect (unlike FRCP 31’s 14, 7, and 7 days), and the court may change these times; the officer then takes the answers and prepares, certifies, and files the deposition.","u":"https://www.supremecourt.ohio.gov/docs/LegalResources/Rules/civil/CivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"32","t":"Use of depositions in court proceedings","s":"A deposition to be used as evidence must be filed at least one day before trial or hearing unless the court allows later filing. Any deposition may be used to impeach, an adverse party’s or its officer’s or designee’s deposition may be used for any purpose, and any witness’s deposition may be used if the witness is dead, outside the court’s subpoena power or resides outside the county, unable to attend from age, illness, or imprisonment, cannot be subpoenaed, is an attending physician or medical expert (even if in the county), or in exceptional circumstances; unlike FRCP 32’s 100-mile test, Ohio uses residence outside the county. Objections to form and curable irregularities are waived if not made at the deposition, objections to written questions must be served within the time for the next round and within 7 days after the last questions, and transcription defects are waived absent a prompt motion to suppress.","u":"https://www.supremecourt.ohio.gov/docs/LegalResources/Rules/civil/CivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"33","t":"Interrogatories to parties","s":"Without leave, a party may serve up to 40 interrogatories on any other party, counting each subpart separately (unlike FRCP 33’s 25 including discrete subparts), preferably electronically in an editable format (plus a paper copy to an unrepresented party), and the responder need answer only the first 40 if more are served. Answers are made separately under oath, quoting each interrogatory before the answer or objection, within the period the requester sets, which may not be less than 28 days after service (FRCP 33 gives 30 days), unless the court changes it. Contention interrogatories are allowed, and a party may answer by specifying business records when the burden of finding the answer is the same for both sides.","u":"https://www.supremecourt.ohio.gov/docs/LegalResources/Rules/civil/CivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"34","t":"Producing documents, ESI, and things; entry onto land","s":"A party may request to inspect, copy, test, or sample documents, ESI, and tangible things in another party’s possession, custody, or control, or to enter land, describing items with reasonable particularity and specifying a time, place, manner, and ESI form, and must serve an electronic, editable copy of the request by e-mail unless excused. The written response is due within the period stated in the request, not less than 28 days after service (FRCP 34 gives 30 days), stating any objections and the ESI form to be used; documents are produced as kept or organized by request category, and ESI need be produced in only one form. Nonparties are reached by Civ.R. 45 subpoena, and, unlike FRCP 34, a person may petition the common pleas court for pre-suit discovery needed to identify a potential adverse party after reasonable efforts to get the information voluntarily.","u":"https://www.supremecourt.ohio.gov/docs/LegalResources/Rules/civil/CivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"35","t":"Physical and mental examinations","s":"When a party’s (or a person in a party’s custody or control) physical or mental condition, including blood group, is in controversy, the court may order an examination on motion for good cause with notice, specifying time, place, manner, conditions, scope, and examiner. On request, the examined party gets the examiner’s detailed written report, and the requesting party is then entitled to reports of other examinations of the same condition; obtaining the report or deposing the examiner waives privilege as to other examiners of that condition, and these rules also apply to examinations by agreement.","u":"https://www.supremecourt.ohio.gov/docs/LegalResources/Rules/civil/CivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"36","t":"Requests for admission","s":"A party may serve requests to admit facts, application of law to fact, or the genuineness of documents, preferably electronically in an editable format (plus a paper copy to an unrepresented party); each matter is admitted unless a written answer or objection is served within the period stated in the request, not less than 28 days after service (FRCP 36 gives 30 days), unless the court changes it. Answers must quote each request, specifically deny or explain why the party cannot admit or deny after reasonable inquiry, and an admission is conclusive in that action only unless the court permits withdrawal. Unique to Ohio, if requests for admission are combined with other discovery, the document’s caption must say so, or no response is required.","u":"https://www.supremecourt.ohio.gov/docs/LegalResources/Rules/civil/CivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"37","t":"Failure to make discovery; sanctions","s":"After certifying a good-faith effort to confer, a party may move to compel answers, designations, interrogatory responses, or inspection, with evasive or incomplete answers treated as failures, and the losing side on the motion generally pays reasonable expenses and attorney fees unless substantially justified. Disobeying a discovery order can lead to facts being taken as established, preclusion, striking pleadings, stays, dismissal, default judgment, or contempt (except for refusing a physical or mental exam), plus mandatory expenses; failing to disclose or supplement bars use of the information or witness unless substantially justified or harmless; failing to take part in the Civ.R. 26(F) conference or a Civ.R. 30(B)(5) conference can bring expenses or other sanctions; and a party who wrongly fails to admit may have to pay the cost of proving the matter. Failing to attend one’s own deposition or respond to interrogatories or inspection requests is sanctionable and not excused by objections absent a pending protective order motion; lost ESI that should have been preserved can be cured by measures no greater than necessary, while an adverse presumption, adverse-inference instruction, dismissal, or default requires a finding of intent to deprive.","u":"https://www.supremecourt.ohio.gov/docs/LegalResources/Rules/civil/CivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"38","t":"Jury trial of right","s":"The right to a jury trial is preserved, but a party must serve a written demand (it may be endorsed on a pleading, captioned “jury demand endorsed hereon”) no later than 14 days after service of the last pleading on the issue; if a demand covers only some issues, another party has 14 days (or less if the court orders) to demand a jury on other issues. A civil jury has 8 members (12 in corporate right-of-way appropriation cases) unless the demand asks for fewer, and if several parties demand, the larger number controls up to that cap. Failing to serve and file a demand waives the jury, and a demand can’t be withdrawn without the parties’ consent. Unlike the FRCP, which allows juries of 6 to 12, Ohio fixes the default at 8.","u":"https://www.supremecourt.ohio.gov/docs/LegalResources/Rules/civil/CivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"39","t":"Trial by jury or by the court","s":"Demanded issues are tried to a jury unless the parties stipulate to a bench trial or the court finds no jury right exists; failing to appear for trial waives the jury. Undemanded issues are tried by the court, though it may order a jury on motion, and in non-jury cases it may use an advisory jury or, with both parties’ consent, a binding jury. A party may ask for a bench trial by live two-way video by the scheduling-order deadline or 30 days before trial; the court can’t hold a remote trial over objection unless a party is limited by active military duty, incarceration, medical restrictions, significant travel distance, or other good cause.","u":"https://www.supremecourt.ohio.gov/docs/LegalResources/Rules/civil/CivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"40","t":"Pre-recorded testimony","s":"All testimony and other appropriate evidence may be presented at trial by video recording, subject to the Rules of Superintendence. Unlike FRCP 40, which deals with scheduling cases for trial, Ohio’s Rule 40 covers prerecorded video trials.","u":"https://www.supremecourt.ohio.gov/docs/LegalResources/Rules/civil/CivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"41","t":"Dismissal of actions","s":"A plaintiff may dismiss without a court order by filing a notice any time before trial begins (unless a counterclaim that can’t stand independently has been served) or by a stipulation signed by all parties who have appeared; such dismissals are without prejudice, but a second notice dismissal of the same claim operates as a judgment on the merits (the “two-dismissal rule”). Otherwise dismissal requires a court order, and the court may dismiss for failure to prosecute or obey rules or orders after notice to plaintiff’s counsel, or after plaintiff rests in a bench trial; involuntary dismissals are on the merits unless the order says otherwise, except dismissals for lack of jurisdiction or failure to join a party under Civ.R. 19 or 19.1. The court may order payment of costs from a previously dismissed action and stay the new one until paid. Unlike FRCP 41(a), which cuts off notice dismissals once an answer or summary-judgment motion is served, Ohio allows them until trial begins.","u":"https://www.supremecourt.ohio.gov/docs/LegalResources/Rules/civil/CivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"42","t":"Consolidation; separate trials","s":"Actions sharing a common question of law or fact may be joined for hearing or trial, consolidated, or otherwise managed to avoid cost and delay, and the court may order separate trials of issues or claims while preserving any jury right. In asbestos, silicosis, and mixed-dust-disease tort cases, actions may be consolidated for case management, but for trial only with all parties’ consent or, absent consent, only actions involving the same exposed person and household members.","u":"https://www.supremecourt.ohio.gov/docs/LegalResources/Rules/civil/CivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"43","t":"Taking testimony","s":"Witnesses testify in open court unless a statute or rule provides otherwise, but for good cause and with safeguards the court may allow remote testimony; a request for remote testimony must be discussed with counsel and unrepresented parties and filed by the scheduling-order deadline or 30 days before the trial or hearing, unless the court allows later notice for good cause. Motions may be heard on affidavits, depositions, or oral testimony. Every remote witness, including out-of-state witnesses, must affirm on the record that they submit to the Ohio court’s jurisdiction for enforcing the oath, including perjury.","u":"https://www.supremecourt.ohio.gov/docs/LegalResources/Rules/civil/CivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"44","t":"Proof of official record","s":"A domestic official record may be proved by an official publication or a copy attested by its custodian with a certificate of custody; a foreign record needs an attested copy plus a final certification of genuineness by a U.S. or foreign diplomatic or consular official, though the court may excuse certification for good cause. A certified statement that a diligent search found no record is admissible to prove absence, and other lawful methods of proof remain available.","u":"https://www.supremecourt.ohio.gov/docs/LegalResources/Rules/civil/CivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"44.1","t":"Judicial notice of law; foreign law","s":"Courts must judicially notice Ohio Supreme Court rules and Ohio case, constitutional, and statutory law. A party relying on an Ohio municipal ordinance, local rule, or administrative regulation, or on the law of another U.S. state or jurisdiction, must give notice in a pleading or other reasonable notice, though a court may notice its own rules and local ordinances without notice. A party relying on foreign-country law must give written notice, and the court may consider any relevant source; all such determinations are rulings of law for the court, not the jury.","u":"https://www.supremecourt.ohio.gov/docs/LegalResources/Rules/civil/CivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"45","t":"Subpoena","s":"A subpoena, issued blank by the clerk or signed by an attorney of record, may command attendance at a trial or hearing anywhere in Ohio, a deposition in the county where the person lives or works (or as ordered), or production or inspection of documents, ESI, things, or premises, but it can’t be used to get a party’s deposition or documents in discovery (use Civ.R. 30 and 34). It may be served by sheriff, bailiff, attorney, or other authorized person, in person, at the residence, or by certified or express mail, with one day’s attendance fee and mileage tendered on demand (without demand if the witness lives outside the county), and other parties get prompt notice of document subpoenas. A person commanded to produce may serve written objections within 14 days after service (or before compliance if sooner), after which production requires a court order; the court must quash or modify subpoenas that allow too little time, seek privileged matter or unretained-expert opinions, or impose undue burden (after a good-faith effort to resolve it). Disobedience may be contempt, and frivolous resistance or burdensome subpoenas can draw sanctions including attorney fees.","u":"https://www.supremecourt.ohio.gov/docs/LegalResources/Rules/civil/CivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"46","t":"Exceptions unnecessary","s":"A formal exception is not needed to preserve an issue for review once the matter has been raised by objection, motion, or otherwise and the court has ruled.","u":"https://www.supremecourt.ohio.gov/docs/LegalResources/Rules/civil/CivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"47","t":"Jurors","s":"Prospective jurors are examined under oath by the court or the parties, and the court may give a brief introduction to the case. Each party (or side, if interests are essentially the same) gets 3 peremptory challenges, exercised alternately starting with the plaintiff; passing waives that challenge only, but if all sides pass in sequence, all remaining peremptories are waived. The court may seat up to 4 alternates, with 1 extra peremptory per party if 1 or 2 alternates are seated and 2 extra if 3 or 4 are seated; alternates may be retained after the jury retires, and deliberations restart if one is substituted. The court may let jurors take notes (destroyed after verdict) and submit written questions to witnesses under listed safeguards.","u":"https://www.supremecourt.ohio.gov/docs/LegalResources/Rules/civil/CivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"48","t":"Majority verdict; number of jurors","s":"A civil verdict requires agreement of three-fourths or more of the jurors, signed in writing by those concurring; on request the jury is polled, and if more than one-fourth disagree the jury deliberates further. Parties may stipulate to fewer jurors than the Civ.R. 38(B) maximum, three-fourths is rounded up to the next whole juror, and juries of fewer than 4 must be unanimous. Unlike FRCP 48, which requires a unanimous verdict unless the parties stipulate otherwise, Ohio allows a three-fourths verdict.","u":"https://www.supremecourt.ohio.gov/docs/LegalResources/Rules/civil/CivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"49","t":"Verdicts; interrogatories","s":"General verdicts are used, and on any party’s request before argument the court must submit written interrogatories along with the general verdict form. If answers and verdict conflict, the court may enter judgment on the answers, send the jury back, or order a new trial. Unlike FRCP 49, Ohio abolishes special verdicts.","u":"https://www.supremecourt.ohio.gov/docs/LegalResources/Rules/civil/CivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"50","t":"Directed verdict; JNOV","s":"A party may move for a directed verdict, stating specific grounds, after the opponent’s opening statement, at the close of the opponent’s evidence, or at the close of all evidence; it is granted when, construing the evidence most strongly for the opponent, reasonable minds could reach only a conclusion adverse to that party. A motion for judgment notwithstanding the verdict (or for judgment if no verdict) must be served within 28 days of entry of judgment (or of the clerk’s actual service if not made within Civ.R. 58(B)’s 3 days), or within 28 days of the jury’s discharge if no verdict; responses are due in 14 days and replies in 7 unless local rule or order says otherwise, and a new-trial motion may be joined. If JNOV is granted the court must also conditionally rule on any new-trial motion, the losing party may move for a new trial within 28 days after the JNOV entry, and the court must state its basis in writing. Ohio keeps the “directed verdict” and “JNOV” names where FRCP 50 uses “judgment as a matter of law.”","u":"https://www.supremecourt.ohio.gov/docs/LegalResources/Rules/civil/CivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"51","t":"Jury instructions; objections","s":"At the close of evidence or earlier as directed, parties may file written requested instructions (copies to all parties), and the court must rule on them before closing arguments, give complete instructions after argument (it may also instruct before), and give the jury at least one written copy or recording of the final instructions. To raise instruction error on appeal, a party must object before the jury retires, stating the matter and grounds specifically, with an opportunity to do so outside the jury’s hearing. The court may also give cautionary instructions during trial.","u":"https://www.supremecourt.ohio.gov/docs/LegalResources/Rules/civil/CivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"52","t":"Findings by the court","s":"In a bench trial, judgment may be general unless a party requests in writing, before entry of judgment or within 7 days after notice of the court’s announced decision (whichever is later), separate findings of fact and conclusions of law. The court may require proposed findings, and findings are not needed on motions such as those under Civ.R. 12, 55, and 56. Unlike FRCP 52, which requires findings in every bench trial, Ohio requires them only on request.","u":"https://www.supremecourt.ohio.gov/docs/LegalResources/Rules/civil/CivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"53","t":"Magistrates","s":"A court may appoint magistrates (lawyers with at least 4 years of practice in good standing) to decide motions, try non-jury cases, preside over jury trials on the parties’ unanimous written consent, handle temporary protection orders, and regulate proceedings, including contempt in their presence by written order subject to immediate judicial review. A party may move to set aside a non-dispositive magistrate’s order within 10 days; findings of fact and conclusions of law must be requested before or within 7 days after a magistrate’s decision is filed, and the clerk serves decisions within 3 days. Objections to a magistrate’s decision are due within 14 days of filing (others may cross-object within 10 days after the first objections), must be specific, and factual objections need a transcript or affidavit filed within 30 days; failing to object waives all but plain error on appeal. A decision is effective only when adopted by the court, which reviews objections independently; a judgment entered during the 14-day window is automatically stayed by timely objections, and interim orders last no more than 28 days, extendable in 28-day increments for good cause. Unlike FRCP 53, which governs special masters, Ohio’s rule creates a broad magistrate system.","u":"https://www.supremecourt.ohio.gov/docs/LegalResources/Rules/civil/CivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"54","t":"Judgments; costs","s":"A “judgment” is a written, judge-signed entry granting or denying relief and journalized on the docket. In multi-claim or multi-party cases, a final judgment on fewer than all claims or parties requires an express finding of “no just reason for delay”; otherwise the order stays revisable. A default judgment can’t exceed or differ in kind from what was demanded, other judgments grant all relief the winner is entitled to, costs go to the prevailing party unless the court directs otherwise, and rule-based attorney-fee awards may reflect the reasonable value of services even if the client didn’t pay.","u":"https://www.supremecourt.ohio.gov/docs/LegalResources/Rules/civil/CivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"55","t":"Default","s":"When a party fails to plead or defend, the opposing party applies to the court for default judgment; no default may be entered against a minor or incompetent person without an appearing guardian or representative, and a defaulting party who has appeared must get written notice at least 7 days before the hearing. The court may hold hearings or a jury trial to fix damages, may set aside a default judgment under Civ.R. 60(B), and may enter default against the state or its subdivisions only on satisfactory evidence. Unlike FRCP 55, Ohio has no clerk’s entry of default or clerk-entered default judgment; application goes to the court.","u":"https://www.supremecourt.ohio.gov/docs/LegalResources/Rules/civil/CivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"56","t":"Summary judgment","s":"A claimant may move for summary judgment once the adverse party’s time to respond to the pleading has expired or after the adverse party moves, and a defending party may move at any time, but if the case is set for pretrial or trial, leave of court is required. Responses and replies follow Civ.R. 6(C), and the court may consider only listed materials (pleadings, depositions, interrogatory answers, admissions, affidavits, transcripts, and stipulations); judgment is granted when there is no genuine issue of material fact and reasonable minds, construing the evidence most strongly for the non-movant, can reach only a conclusion adverse to that party, and it may be granted on liability alone. The opposing party can’t rest on the pleadings but must set out specific facts, affidavits must be on personal knowledge, the court may grant a continuance for discovery when facts are unavailable, and bad-faith affidavits draw expenses, attorney fees, and possible contempt. Unlike FRCP 56, which lets a motion be filed until 30 days after discovery closes, Ohio requires leave once the case is set for pretrial or trial.","u":"https://www.supremecourt.ohio.gov/docs/LegalResources/Rules/civil/CivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"57","t":"Declaratory judgments","s":"Declaratory judgment actions under R.C. Chapter 2721 follow these rules; another adequate remedy doesn’t bar declaratory relief, and the court may advance the hearing on the trial list.","u":"https://www.supremecourt.ohio.gov/docs/LegalResources/Rules/civil/CivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"58","t":"Entry of judgment","s":"After a general verdict, an announced decision, or a grant or denial of relief, the court must promptly prepare (or have counsel prepare) a judgment entry and file and journalize it within 30 days absent good cause; a judgment is effective only when the clerk enters it on the journal, and approving an entry doesn’t waive appeal objections. Within 3 days of journalizing, the clerk must serve notice on all parties not in default for failure to appear, but failure to serve doesn’t affect validity or appeal time except under App.R. 4(A). Entry isn’t delayed for taxing costs.","u":"https://www.supremecourt.ohio.gov/docs/LegalResources/Rules/civil/CivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"59","t":"New trials","s":"A new trial may be granted for irregularity, misconduct, accident or surprise, excessive or inadequate damages from passion or prejudice, error in amount of recovery, weight of the evidence (only once per case), judgment contrary to law, newly discovered evidence, error of law at trial, or other good cause, with grounds stated in writing. Motions for new trial, remittitur, additur, prejudgment interest, or attorney fees must be served within 28 days of entry of judgment (or of the clerk’s actual service if not done within 3 days); responses are due in 14 days and replies in 7 unless local rule or order says otherwise. Opposing affidavits are due 14 days after service, extendable up to 21 more days, and the court on its own may order a new trial within 28 days after judgment.","u":"https://www.supremecourt.ohio.gov/docs/LegalResources/Rules/civil/CivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"60","t":"Relief from judgment","s":"Clerical mistakes may be corrected any time (with appellate leave once an appeal is docketed). On motion, a court may relieve a party from a final judgment for (1) mistake, inadvertence, surprise, or excusable neglect; (2) newly discovered evidence; (3) fraud or misconduct; (4) satisfaction, reversal of an underlying judgment, or inequity of prospective application; or (5) any other justifying reason; the motion must be filed within a reasonable time and, for grounds (1) through (3), within 1 year. The motion doesn’t suspend the judgment. Unlike FRCP 60(b), Ohio has no separate ground for void judgments.","u":"https://www.supremecourt.ohio.gov/docs/LegalResources/Rules/civil/CivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"61","t":"Harmless error","s":"No error in admitting or excluding evidence or in any ruling justifies a new trial or disturbing a judgment unless refusing relief would be inconsistent with substantial justice; errors not affecting substantial rights are disregarded.","u":"https://www.supremecourt.ohio.gov/docs/LegalResources/Rules/civil/CivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"62","t":"Stay of judgment","s":"On motion any time after judgment, the court may stay execution, on security conditions, until the time to move under Civ.R. 50, 59, or 60 or to appeal runs, and while such motions are pending. An appellant obtains a stay pending appeal by posting an adequate supersedeas bond, effective when the court approves it; the state and its subdivisions, agencies, and officers need no bond. Appellate courts keep their own stay powers, and Rule 54(B) partial judgments may be stayed. Unlike FRCP 62(a), Ohio has no automatic 30-day stay after judgment.","u":"https://www.supremecourt.ohio.gov/docs/LegalResources/Rules/civil/CivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"63","t":"Disability of a judge","s":"If the trial judge can’t continue a jury trial, or can’t perform post-verdict or post-findings duties, another judge designated by the administrative judge (or by the Chief Justice for a single-judge division) may take over after certifying familiarity with the record, or may grant a new trial if unable to do so.","u":"https://www.supremecourt.ohio.gov/docs/LegalResources/Rules/civil/CivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"64","t":"Seizure of person or property","s":"All remedies provided by law for seizing a person or property to secure a future judgment, such as arrest, attachment, garnishment, replevin, and sequestration, are available at the start of and during an action.","u":"https://www.supremecourt.ohio.gov/docs/LegalResources/Rules/civil/CivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"65","t":"Injunctions","s":"A temporary restraining order may issue without notice only on specific sworn facts showing immediate irreparable harm and the attorney’s written certification of notice efforts; it expires in no more than 14 days unless extended once for a like period for good cause or longer by consent, the preliminary-injunction hearing must be set at the earliest possible time, and the adverse party may move to dissolve it on 2 days’ notice. A preliminary injunction requires reasonable notice and may be consolidated with trial on the merits. No TRO or preliminary injunction is operative until the applicant posts a court-fixed bond with surety or a cash or equivalent deposit (none required of the state or its subdivisions), and every order must state reasons and describe the restrained acts specifically; ex parte orders are served as process under Civ.R. 4 to 4.6.","u":"https://www.supremecourt.ohio.gov/docs/LegalResources/Rules/civil/CivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"65.1","t":"Civil protection orders","s":"Governs domestic violence, dating violence, stalking, and sexually oriented offense civil protection order proceedings under R.C. 3113.31, 2151.34, and 2903.214, overriding inconsistent civil rules. The clerk serves the petition and any ex parte order by personal service (later papers under Civ.R. 5(B)), and service may be modified to protect the petitioner’s address; discovery is allowed only by court order addressing time, place, attendees (including a victim advocate), and the petitioner’s safety; the court may appoint counsel for a minor respondent. Magistrates may grant or deny ex parte orders directly, and full-hearing orders take effect when adopted by the court; objections are due within 14 days of the court’s filing of the order (others may cross-object within 10 days after), don’t stay the order, and factual objections need a transcript or affidavit within 30 days. The adopted order is final and appealable, but objections must be filed first and toll the appeal time; the court must send NCIC notice forms to law enforcement, a Form 9 notice of an existing order goes back to the issuing clerk within 3 business days, non-merits dismissals don’t bar refiling, and records that could reveal a petitioner’s identity or location are excluded from remote public access.","u":"https://www.supremecourt.ohio.gov/docs/LegalResources/Rules/civil/CivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"66","t":"Receivers","s":"An action in which a receiver has been appointed can be dismissed only by court order, and receiverships are administered as provided by law and court rules.","u":"https://www.supremecourt.ohio.gov/docs/LegalResources/Rules/civil/CivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"67","t":"Reserved","s":"Reserved.","u":"https://www.supremecourt.ohio.gov/docs/LegalResources/Rules/civil/CivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"68","t":"Offer of judgment","s":"A refused offer of judgment can’t be filed with the court for purposes of determining costs, though voluntary settlement offers remain allowed. Unlike FRCP 68, Ohio’s rule has no cost-shifting penalty for rejecting an offer.","u":"https://www.supremecourt.ohio.gov/docs/LegalResources/Rules/civil/CivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"69","t":"Execution","s":"A money judgment is enforced by writ of execution unless the court directs otherwise, with procedures as provided by law, and the judgment creditor may take discovery from anyone, including the debtor, under these rules in aid of the judgment.","u":"https://www.supremecourt.ohio.gov/docs/LegalResources/Rules/civil/CivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"70","t":"Judgment for specific acts; vesting title","s":"If a party fails to perform a specific act ordered by judgment (such as conveying land or delivering documents) within the time set, the court may have another person do it at that party’s cost, issue a writ of attachment, hold the party in contempt, or for property in Ohio enter a judgment transferring title directly; a party entitled to possession may obtain a writ of execution from the clerk.","u":"https://www.supremecourt.ohio.gov/docs/LegalResources/Rules/civil/CivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"71","t":"Process for and against nonparties","s":"A nonparty in whose favor an order is made may enforce it as if a party, and a nonparty against whom an order is lawfully enforceable is subject to the same enforcement process as a party.","u":"https://www.supremecourt.ohio.gov/docs/LegalResources/Rules/civil/CivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"72","t":"Reserved","s":"Reserved.","u":"https://www.supremecourt.ohio.gov/docs/LegalResources/Rules/civil/CivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"73","t":"Probate division","s":"Rules 73 to 73.29 apply only in the probate division and control over conflicting civil rules; venue follows law rather than Civ.R. 3(C), and improperly venued cases are transferred with possible costs and attorney fees against the filer. Summons service follows Civ.R. 4 to 4.6; other required notice may be given by personal or residence delivery, certified or express mail or signed-receipt commercial carrier, ordinary mail after refusal or unclaimed return, publication once a week for 3 consecutive weeks (after an affidavit that the person can’t be found), or as the court directs, with proof by affidavit. Probate filings need no oath, standard forms under Civ.R. 73.2 and 73.3 apply, Civ.R. 58(B) notice of judgment applies when a responsive pleading or exceptions were filed, and the probate judge is ex officio clerk and may allow electronic filing by local rule.","u":"https://www.supremecourt.ohio.gov/docs/LegalResources/Rules/civil/CivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"73.1","t":"Definitions","s":"Defines a probate “case” to include civil complaints and administrative appeals, estates, trusts, guardianships, conservatorships, emergency orders, adoptions, name changes, and any other matter given a case number (such as will filings, minor settlements, birth corrections, commitments, and powers of attorney); each wrongful-death trust beneficiary, ward, adoptee, or name-change applicant counts as a separate case.","u":"https://www.supremecourt.ohio.gov/docs/LegalResources/Rules/civil/CivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"73.2","t":"Standard probate forms","s":"Prescribes the Supreme Court’s Standard Probate Forms for designated probate filings; where none exists, Civil Rule or local forms are used. Forms may be neatly modified by deletion or interlineation (courts can’t require routine modification), and original instruments may be prepared on 8½ by 11 inch paper following the standard caption and format.","u":"https://www.supremecourt.ohio.gov/docs/LegalResources/Rules/civil/CivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"73.3","t":"Printing specifications for probate forms","s":"Sets printing specifications for blank probate forms (estates, guardianships, adoptions, and related matters): pages no larger than 8½ by 11 inches, side margins of about ½ to ¾ inch and top margin of about ⅞ to 1⅛ inches, sans-serif type without italics, main headings in 16-point or larger, captions and subheadings in 12 to 16 points, body in 8 to 12 points, and under-line instructions no larger than 8 points. It also fixes one-pica vertical spacing, minimum blank-line lengths (8 pica for a county or dollar amount, 18 for a date, 20 for a name, 40 for a one-line address), check boxes, standard captions, the form number repeated at the foot of page 1 no higher than ⅜ inch from the bottom, and limited permitted variations.","u":"https://www.supremecourt.ohio.gov/docs/LegalResources/Rules/civil/CivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"73.4","t":"Hours of the court","s":"Each probate court sets its own business hours.","u":"https://www.supremecourt.ohio.gov/docs/LegalResources/Rules/civil/CivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"73.5","t":"Conduct in the court","s":"Conduct interfering with court business is prohibited, and no recording, broadcasting, or photography (other than by the court reporter) is allowed without the court’s advance consent under Sup.R. 11.00.","u":"https://www.supremecourt.ohio.gov/docs/LegalResources/Rules/civil/CivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"73.6","t":"Examination of probate records","s":"Records can’t be removed from the court without the judge’s approval, copies cost a judge-set per-page fee, and adoption, mental illness, minor settlement, and similar confidential records are accessible only as the judge authorizes, with contempt for unauthorized removal or disclosure.","u":"https://www.supremecourt.ohio.gov/docs/LegalResources/Rules/civil/CivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"73.7","t":"Continuances","s":"Continuance motions must be in writing with caption and case number and a proposed entry leaving the new date blank, and are granted only on proof of reasonable notice to or consent of the adverse party; failing to object within a reasonable time counts as consent.","u":"https://www.supremecourt.ohio.gov/docs/LegalResources/Rules/civil/CivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"73.8","t":"Filings and judgment entries","s":"Filings (except wills) must be on 8½ by 11 inch microfilmable paper, typed or printed and captioned, with the case number on each page and counsel’s (or the fiduciary’s) contact information and registration number. A fiduciary who fails to keep an address current may be removed after at least 10 days’ written notice of the hearing; the prevailing party’s counsel must submit a proposed judgment entry within 7 days after judgment, and opposing counsel has 7 days to object.","u":"https://www.supremecourt.ohio.gov/docs/LegalResources/Rules/civil/CivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"73.9","t":"Deposit for court costs","s":"A deposit in the amount set by local rule is required when any action or proceeding is filed, additional deposits may be required, and the deposit is applied as filings occur.","u":"https://www.supremecourt.ohio.gov/docs/LegalResources/Rules/civil/CivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"73.10","t":"Wills","s":"Before applying to probate a will, appoint a fiduciary, or relieve an estate from administration, the applicant or attorney must check the index of deposited wills, and earlier deposited wills are filed for record only. The fiduciary of a testate estate must file a Certificate of Service of Notice of Probate of Will (Form 2.4) within 2 months of appointment or face removal; a minor can’t sign a waiver of notice, nor can one be signed on behalf of a minor aged 16 or 17.","u":"https://www.supremecourt.ohio.gov/docs/LegalResources/Rules/civil/CivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"73.11","t":"Letters of authority and notice of appointment","s":"Notice of an application to appoint an administrator must be served at least 7 days before the hearing (on court-designated persons if there is no known Ohio spouse or next of kin). The administrator must notify all heirs and support-allowance claimants within 7 days after appointment unless they had notice of the hearing or waived it, and the court must serve the surviving spouse with the spousal citation and summary of rights within 7 days of the initial appointment unless local rule sets a different time.","u":"https://www.supremecourt.ohio.gov/docs/LegalResources/Rules/civil/CivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"73.12","t":"Appraisers","s":"A fiduciary may pay an appraiser a reasonable agreed fee up to the local-rule limit without applying to the court (court approval is required if there is no local rule), and a specially qualified appraiser may be appointed for unusual property.","u":"https://www.supremecourt.ohio.gov/docs/LegalResources/Rules/civil/CivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"73.13","t":"Claims against estate","s":"The fiduciary must file a copy of any rejection of a claim filed with the court, and if a claims or insolvency hearing is held, must file a schedule of all claims with the hearing application or within 10 days after the court notifies the fiduciary of a court-initiated hearing.","u":"https://www.supremecourt.ohio.gov/docs/LegalResources/Rules/civil/CivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"73.14","t":"Sale of personal property","s":"An application to sell personal property must adequately describe it, and except for good cause no order of sale issues before the inventory is filed.","u":"https://www.supremecourt.ohio.gov/docs/LegalResources/Rules/civil/CivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"73.15","t":"Accounts","s":"Vouchers and receipts must be keyed to the account by number, letter, or date; land sales must show gross proceeds with the closing statement; receipts signed under a power of attorney require the recorded power to be attached. The court may require assets to be exhibited with a partial account (by bank statement, certified inventory, or examination by a deputy clerk or commissioner), and a final or distributive account isn’t approved until all court costs are paid.","u":"https://www.supremecourt.ohio.gov/docs/LegalResources/Rules/civil/CivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"73.16","t":"Land sales","s":"In R.C. Chapter 2127 land sales, the plaintiff must file evidence of title dated after the complaint before the court finds the sale necessary, and must mail notice of a public sale to all defendants at least 3 weeks beforehand and certify notice and advertising before the sale. Private-sale confirmation entries must show gross proceeds and the proposed closing statement, and the court may appoint a disinterested investigator whose fee is taxed as costs.","u":"https://www.supremecourt.ohio.gov/docs/LegalResources/Rules/civil/CivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"73.17","t":"Estates of minors up to $25,000","s":"Applications for minors’ estates of not more than $25,000 are brought by the parent or custodian in the minor’s name and must state the amount and payee. If no guardian is appointed, the minor’s attorney prepares an entry depositing the funds in the minor’s name, impounding principal and interest until court order or majority, deposits the funds, and files a Verification of Receipt and Deposit (Form 22.3) within 7 days of the entry.","u":"https://www.supremecourt.ohio.gov/docs/LegalResources/Rules/civil/CivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"73.18","t":"Settlement of minors’ injury claims","s":"The guardian of the estate, or if none the parents or custodian, applies in the minor’s name to settle a minor’s injury claim; a noncustodial parent is entitled to 7 days’ notice, which may be waived. The application needs a current physician’s statement on the injuries, disclosure of payments to others and of the fee arrangement (fees need court approval), and the minor and applicant must attend the hearing.","u":"https://www.supremecourt.ohio.gov/docs/LegalResources/Rules/civil/CivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"73.19","t":"Settlement of adult wards’ claims","s":"The guardian of the estate applies to settle claims for or against an adult ward, with notice to interested parties as the court determines (the court may dispense with notice), and the court may authorize settlement in the ward’s best interest. Injury settlements need a current physician’s statement and disclosure of payments to others and of counsel fees, which require court approval.","u":"https://www.supremecourt.ohio.gov/docs/LegalResources/Rules/civil/CivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"73.20","t":"Wrongful death and survival settlements","s":"An application to approve settlement and distribution (Form 14.0) must state the facts, the amounts allocated to the wrongful-death and survival claims, and the proposed distribution of wrongful-death proceeds. The fiduciary must give written notice and a copy to interested persons who haven’t waived, the court keeps jurisdiction despite waivers, and counsel fees need court approval.","u":"https://www.supremecourt.ohio.gov/docs/LegalResources/Rules/civil/CivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"73.21","t":"Counsel fees","s":"Probate attorney fees are governed by Prof.Cond.R. 1.5, aren’t paid in estates until the final account is prepared unless the court approves earlier, and require a written application and hearing unless local rule provides otherwise, with notice to affected parties. No fees are allowed to counsel for fiduciaries delinquent in filing accounts absent good cause, there are no automatically approved minimum or maximum fees, and a fiduciary must apply before entering a contingent-fee contract unless local rule says otherwise.","u":"https://www.supremecourt.ohio.gov/docs/LegalResources/Rules/civil/CivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"73.22","t":"Executor and administrator commissions","s":"Extra compensation for extraordinary services requires an itemized application, possibly with a hearing. The court may deny or reduce commissions for late inventories or accounts or unfaithful service; co-fiduciaries together get no more than one would (unless the will provides otherwise), and commissions are reduced by counsel fees paid for the fiduciary’s own work absent good cause.","u":"https://www.supremecourt.ohio.gov/docs/LegalResources/Rules/civil/CivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"73.23","t":"Guardian’s compensation","s":"Guardian compensation is set by local rule, expenses must be itemized and not exceed court-approved amounts, and extra compensation or person-only guardian fees require an itemized application, possibly with a hearing. Co-guardians together get no more than one guardian would, and compensation may be denied or reduced for delinquent filings or unfaithful service.","u":"https://www.supremecourt.ohio.gov/docs/LegalResources/Rules/civil/CivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"73.24","t":"Trustee’s compensation","s":"Trustee compensation is set by local rule, with extraordinary-service fees on itemized application; co-trustees together get no more than one would unless the instrument provides otherwise. Absent good cause, no trustee compensation or counsel fees are allowed while an account is delinquent, and compensation may be reduced for delinquency or unfaithful service.","u":"https://www.supremecourt.ohio.gov/docs/LegalResources/Rules/civil/CivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"73.25","t":"Local rules","s":"Local probate rules must be numbered to match the rule they supplement (for example, Local Rule 73.12).","u":"https://www.supremecourt.ohio.gov/docs/LegalResources/Rules/civil/CivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"73.26","t":"Exception to the rules","s":"For good cause on application, the probate division may grant exceptions to Rules 73 to 73.29.","u":"https://www.supremecourt.ohio.gov/docs/LegalResources/Rules/civil/CivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"73.27","t":"Compliance","s":"Failure to follow the probate rules may result in sanctions as the court directs.","u":"https://www.supremecourt.ohio.gov/docs/LegalResources/Rules/civil/CivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"73.28","t":"Probate case management","s":"Fiduciaries must meet statutory or court-ordered deadlines for inventories, accounts, and guardian’s reports, enforced by citation against fiduciary and attorney and by reduced commissions or fees. An estate open more than 6 months under R.C. 2109.301(B)(1) needs an application to extend administration (Form 13.8), extension applications must be signed by the fiduciary, and a status report is due in estates open 13 months after appointment and annually thereafter. Delinquent fiduciaries’ attorneys may be cited and barred from new appointments, and exceptions to an inventory or account must be set for pretrial within 30 days.","u":"https://www.supremecourt.ohio.gov/docs/LegalResources/Rules/civil/CivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"73.29","t":"Dispute resolution","s":"Probate courts may encourage alternative dispute resolution, including suggesting settlement talks, referring parties to eldercaring coordination or mediation, referring cases to early neutral evaluation with a stay of up to 60 days, or, on all parties’ written agreement, any other ADR method.","u":"https://www.supremecourt.ohio.gov/docs/LegalResources/Rules/civil/CivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"74","t":"Reserved","s":"Reserved.","u":"https://www.supremecourt.ohio.gov/docs/LegalResources/Rules/civil/CivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"75","t":"Divorce, annulment, and legal separation","s":"The civil rules apply with modifications: Civ.R. 14, 19, 19.1, and 24 don’t apply, but property holders, a child (with a guardian ad litem), persons claiming custody rights, and a support obligor’s employer may be joined. There is no jury; issues are heard by the judge or a magistrate; Civ.R. 55 default doesn’t apply; and judgment isn’t final under Civ.R. 54(B) unless property, spousal support, and parental rights are also resolved (or the court lacks jurisdiction or, in legal separation, division is inappropriate). Custody investigation reports are available on written request at least 7 days before trial; no case may be heard until 42 days after service or 28 days after last publication, nor within 28 days after service of a counterclaim unless the plaintiff waives; unrepresented adverse parties get notice of trial by mail at least 7 days before trial; and a divorce can’t rest on uncorroborated party testimony. Temporary support and custody orders may be granted on affidavit without hearing, counter-affidavits are due within 14 days, and a requested oral hearing must be held within 28 days; TROs against dissipating property or abuse may issue without notice in place of Civ.R. 65(A) and last through the case. Post-decree motions require Civ.R. 4 service, the court may delay the decree until support or custody is secured, relief pending appeal is sought first in the trial court, a domestic-violence CPO is a separate claim, and non-merits dismissals don’t bar refiling.","u":"https://www.supremecourt.ohio.gov/docs/LegalResources/Rules/civil/CivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"76","t":"Reserved","s":"Reserved (Rules 77 to 80 are also reserved).","u":"https://www.supremecourt.ohio.gov/docs/LegalResources/Rules/civil/CivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"81","t":"References to Ohio Revised Code","s":"A reference to a Revised Code section means that section as amended over time, including later-enacted sections numbered after it.","u":"https://www.supremecourt.ohio.gov/docs/LegalResources/Rules/civil/CivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"82","t":"Jurisdiction unaffected","s":"These rules neither extend nor limit the jurisdiction of Ohio courts.","u":"https://www.supremecourt.ohio.gov/docs/LegalResources/Rules/civil/CivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"83","t":"Local rules","s":"Courts may adopt local practice rules consistent with Supreme Court rules, must file them with the Clerk of the Supreme Court, and must give notice and an opportunity for comment before adoption (or promptly afterward if an immediate need required adopting without it).","u":"https://www.supremecourt.ohio.gov/docs/LegalResources/Rules/civil/CivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"84","t":"Forms","s":"Forms in the Appendix of Forms approved by the Supreme Court are sufficient and must be accepted for filing, and substantially similar local forms may also be accepted. Unlike the FRCP, which abrogated its official forms and Rule 84 in 2015, Ohio keeps an appendix of forms (general civil, domestic relations, civil protection order, and probate).","u":"https://www.supremecourt.ohio.gov/docs/LegalResources/Rules/civil/CivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"85","t":"Title","s":"These rules are the Ohio Rules of Civil Procedure and may be cited as “Civil Rules” or “Civ.R.”","u":"https://www.supremecourt.ohio.gov/docs/LegalResources/Rules/civil/CivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"86","t":"Effective date","s":"The rules took effect July 1, 1970, and amendments filed with the General Assembly take effect the following July 1 unless disapproved, applying to new and pending cases unless that would be infeasible or unjust. The rule itself lists no amendment dates; the most recent amendments in this compilation took effect July 1, 2026.","u":"https://www.supremecourt.ohio.gov/docs/LegalResources/Rules/civil/CivilProcedure.pdf"}]});
